1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,440

2
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:05,940
Great.

3
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:09,080
So we had some feedback and
office hours and other times

4
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,800
that it might be helpful
to have a high-level recap

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,280
of the algorithms that
we've talked about so far

6
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,400
to try to understand where some
of the pieces fit together.

7
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,780
So I put together one slide that
is a little bit more high level

8
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:23,480
but actually captures
almost all of the algorithms

9
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,240
that we've seen so far.

10
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,840
And in particular, we've
talked about both online

11
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:34,280
and offline algorithms, where
online refers to scenarios where

12
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:37,600
the policy will be
used to collect data.

13
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,520
And offline means that
we're given a data set.

14
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:43,560
And we cannot do any
additional data collection

15
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:45,520
in our environment.

16
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,720
And in the offline
setting, we talked

17
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,200
about two different
classes of reinforcement

18
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,320
learning methods that
use on-policy data

19
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:55,400
collection in order to improve.

20
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:57,660
We referred to the first
type as on-policy RL

21
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:00,070
and the second type
as off-policy RL,

22
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:03,830
where on-policy RL, you
can collect data but only

23
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:06,013
from the current policy.

24
00:01:06,013 --> 00:01:07,930
You can only use data
from the current policy.

25
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:12,220
And this means that you are not
going to be reusing past data.

26
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:15,430
You can't use offline
data and so forth.

27
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:18,750
And this include algorithms
REINFORCE or the vanilla policy

28
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:20,670
gradient method.

29
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:22,890
And then within the
realm of off-policy RL,

30
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:25,730
this was where we could start to
reuse data from other policies,

31
00:01:25,730 --> 00:01:29,430
including data from policies
before we took a gradient step

32
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:32,710
on our policy parameters.

33
00:01:32,710 --> 00:01:35,150
And this included
algorithms like PPO

34
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:37,572
and importance sampling.

35
00:01:37,572 --> 00:01:39,030
And then we talked
about algorithms

36
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:42,830
that are more off-policy that
can reuse data from policies

37
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:46,710
that are even more
different and where

38
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:48,710
we use a replay buffer
to store the data

39
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:50,170
from those past policies.

40
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:53,360
And that included
algorithms like DQN and SAC.

41
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:56,350

42
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,610
And then I colored these
different algorithms

43
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:02,150
different colors because
the blue algorithms only

44
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:03,790
involve training a policy.

45
00:02:03,790 --> 00:02:07,070
So we refer to them as
policy gradient methods.

46
00:02:07,070 --> 00:02:10,270
PPO and SAC learn both
a policy and a critic.

47
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:12,950
And so we refer to them
as actor critic methods.

48
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:16,350
And there's also algorithms
that only learn a critic

49
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:19,030
and don't explicitly
learn a separate policy.

50
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:22,470
And those are often referred
to as Q-learning algorithms,

51
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:24,055
such as DQN.

52
00:02:24,055 --> 00:02:26,430
I should also mention that
PPO is often actually referred

53
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:29,550
to as a policy gradient method,
although you do actually

54
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,450
learn a value function.

55
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,150
And so we typically
were referring to it

56
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:37,750
as actor critic in this
class to avoid confusion.

57
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:40,730
And then separate from these
classes of online methods,

58
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:45,190
we also have offline RL methods
that we started talking about.

59
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:48,510
And these built upon a lot of
the ideas we saw in online RL.

60
00:02:48,510 --> 00:02:50,510
But I have to cope with
the fact that you're not

61
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:52,730
collecting any data at all.

62
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:54,730
And we looked at two
different approaches there.

63
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:58,940
One is to only considering
actions in the data.

64
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:05,660
And this included algorithms
like AWR, AWAC, and IQL and also

65
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:08,380
an algorithm like SQL that
implicitly is constraining you

66
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:13,160
to actions in the data
but not so explicitly.

67
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:16,740
And this is through
conservatism.

68
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:21,980
Now, for these RL algorithms,
generally, as you move right,

69
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:24,180
you need more online data.

70
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:27,020
And so that means
that typically they're

71
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:29,780
going to be more expensive
because online data is often

72
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:33,460
more expensive than offline
data because it's harder

73
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:39,940
to repeatedly collect a
large amount of online data.

74
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:41,880
And then lastly, before
we talked about RL,

75
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:44,180
we also talked about
imitation learning algorithms.

76
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:48,820
And this can also fall
within the bucket of offline

77
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:50,700
and online, although
there's a bit

78
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:52,138
different set of assumptions.

79
00:03:52,138 --> 00:03:54,180
The first imitation learning
algorithm we covered

80
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:57,415
was kind of vanilla
behavior cloning, where

81
00:03:57,415 --> 00:03:59,290
we're taking a data set
and trying to imitate

82
00:03:59,290 --> 00:04:01,410
the actions in that data set.

83
00:04:01,410 --> 00:04:03,730
And then we also talked
about an online algorithm

84
00:04:03,730 --> 00:04:06,970
that is online both
in terms of the policy

85
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:09,850
and in terms of
querying an expert,

86
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:12,130
like asking an expert
what is the right action

87
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:15,290
on this new data
that I'm collecting.

88
00:04:15,290 --> 00:04:16,950
And I put these in--

89
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:18,870
they're in a little bit
of a separate category

90
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:21,070
because they have
different assumptions.

91
00:04:21,070 --> 00:04:23,650
You don't need a
reward function.

92
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:26,090
But you do need
pretty good data.

93
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:28,650
And you're not going to
be able to outperform

94
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:33,190
the average behavior
in your data set.

95
00:04:33,190 --> 00:04:33,690
Cool.

96
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:37,210
So that's an overview including
a lot of these different terms

97
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:39,010
that we see in
reinforcement learning,

98
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:43,250
like offline, online,
off-policy, on-policy,

99
00:04:43,250 --> 00:04:44,850
and so forth, and
imitation learning

100
00:04:44,850 --> 00:04:47,730
versus reinforcement learning.

101
00:04:47,730 --> 00:04:48,730
Cool.

102
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:50,450
So what we're going
to talk about today

103
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:52,700
is we're going to talk about
model-based reinforcement

104
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:53,450
learning.

105
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:56,090
And model-based
reinforcement learning

106
00:04:56,090 --> 00:04:59,170
can actually be applied to
all of the different classes

107
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:02,785
of reinforcement learning
methods on the previous slide.

108
00:05:02,785 --> 00:05:04,410
Someone has a special
place in my heart

109
00:05:04,410 --> 00:05:07,450
because I used to do
actually quite a bit of work

110
00:05:07,450 --> 00:05:08,363
on model-based RL.

111
00:05:08,363 --> 00:05:10,530
And I think that there's a
lot of really cool things

112
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:13,370
that you can do with model-based
reinforcement learning.

113
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:17,230
We'll talk about the key idea
behind it, how to learn models,

114
00:05:17,230 --> 00:05:20,370
how to use those models,
including with planning and data

115
00:05:20,370 --> 00:05:21,490
generation.

116
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:24,490
And then we'll also look at one
case study in dexterous robot

117
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:25,810
manipulation.

118
00:05:25,810 --> 00:05:28,475
And as a teaser for
that, the methods

119
00:05:28,475 --> 00:05:29,850
that we'll be
talking about today

120
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:33,315
is we'll allow a robot to
do something like this,

121
00:05:33,315 --> 00:05:34,190
which is pretty cool.

122
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:35,730
This hand has more
than 20 degrees

123
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:37,730
of freedom, more than 20
different joints that's

124
00:05:37,730 --> 00:05:40,130
controlling.

125
00:05:40,130 --> 00:05:42,547
And then the key
outcomes is trying

126
00:05:42,547 --> 00:05:44,130
to understand how
we can learn and use

127
00:05:44,130 --> 00:05:46,410
dynamics models and the
different challenges

128
00:05:46,410 --> 00:05:51,530
and trade-offs with this
whole class of methods.

129
00:05:51,530 --> 00:05:52,720
Cool.

130
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,720
So the key idea behind
model-based reinforcement

131
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,960
learning is
essentially we're going

132
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:59,800
to try to learn a simulator.

133
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:04,560
Can we learn how to predict
the future for actions

134
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:07,200
that we might be taking?

135
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:12,720
And for robotics and driving
and other physical systems,

136
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,640
this might be
modeling the physics

137
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:18,640
of some particular system
if you can directly

138
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:24,320
observe the underlying physical
entities in that system.

139
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:27,040
And alternatively,
if you can't directly

140
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,000
observe the different entities
in that physical system

141
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and model them, then
this might look something

142
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,920
more like video prediction
conditioned on actions

143
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:37,880
that the agent wants to take.

144
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:40,560
So for example, I
might want to predict--

145
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:44,500
if I say something to someone
else, how will they react?

146
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,780
Or if I, for example, move
my legs in a certain way,

147
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:48,780
will I fall down?

148
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:49,580
Will I go that way?

149
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:51,000
Will I go that way.

150
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:51,840
And so forth.

151
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:54,684

152
00:06:54,684 --> 00:06:57,040
In terms of what this video
production might actually

153
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:01,320
look like, we've seen pretty
cool videos coming out

154
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:04,720
of large models
like Veo 2 and Sora.

155
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:10,640
And these are examples
of videos that are

156
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:12,360
generated by a neural network.

157
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:14,400
You could imagine that
these kinds of models

158
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:19,740
could be very useful for
training systems like cars,

159
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:25,080
robots, quadrotors that are
flying through different places

160
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,550
in order to learn about how
their actions affect the world.

161
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:30,600

162
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,783
Now, models could look
something like models

163
00:07:32,783 --> 00:07:33,700
of the physical world.

164
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,658
They could also look at
models of other things.

165
00:07:35,658 --> 00:07:38,560
So for example, if you
wanted to learn a policy

166
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:40,620
to make money on
the stock market,

167
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:42,880
then one of these
simulators would

168
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,600
be trying to predict what
will happen to the stock

169
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:51,430
market over time and if your
actions have any effect on that.

170
00:07:51,430 --> 00:07:54,550
And then if you're trying to
train an agent to play a game,

171
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:57,410
then this sort of
model will underscore

172
00:07:57,410 --> 00:07:59,470
the rules of that game.

173
00:07:59,470 --> 00:08:00,970
And actually, in a
lot of games, you

174
00:08:00,970 --> 00:08:03,093
might actually already
know the rules of the game.

175
00:08:03,093 --> 00:08:04,510
And you may not
even need to learn

176
00:08:04,510 --> 00:08:05,990
a model in the first place.

177
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:08,310
For example, in chess, if
you take a particular action,

178
00:08:08,310 --> 00:08:09,420
you know the outcome.

179
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:13,550
You know how the piece will
move as a result of that action.

180
00:08:13,550 --> 00:08:15,750
And then it comes down to--

181
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:18,150
in two player games or three
player games or multiplayer

182
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:22,297
games, you might need
to model other players.

183
00:08:22,297 --> 00:08:24,630
Modeling other players-- you
can sometimes consider that

184
00:08:24,630 --> 00:08:25,650
as part of the dynamics.

185
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:28,590
But oftentimes, it's
actually modeled separately.

186
00:08:28,590 --> 00:08:31,310
So you don't even
necessarily need

187
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:34,750
to consider it as part of
this simulator of the world.

188
00:08:34,750 --> 00:08:36,470
And for example, you
might be able to use

189
00:08:36,470 --> 00:08:40,239
your own model of yourself
as a model of another agent.

190
00:08:40,239 --> 00:08:43,049

191
00:08:43,049 --> 00:08:46,710
So if we can learn a
simulator, how would we use it?

192
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:49,180
So the sketch of
an algorithm would

193
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:52,380
be something like this,
where we get some data.

194
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:55,660
Then we learn a simulator
on that data that's

195
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:59,580
going to trying to
predict the future

196
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,860
for the present and
some action that I

197
00:09:02,860 --> 00:09:06,180
might take from the present.

198
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:08,300
And then try to run
your favorite method

199
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:10,520
inside the simulator.

200
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:13,300

201
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:16,700
So for example, maybe you
have a simple scenario,

202
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,380
where you want to get from the
green point to the red point.

203
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:23,020
You might collect some data.

204
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:25,740
This is collected
from some policy.

205
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:29,060
Then you would train some
neural network conditioned

206
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:31,640
on a state and an action.

207
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:34,180
What is the next state?

208
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:36,180
And one way that you
could learn this is just

209
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:39,620
by minimizing the
L2 loss between

210
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:42,540
your predicted next
state and the next state

211
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:45,020
observed in the data.

212
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:48,360
And then in terms of running
your favorite RL method,

213
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,820
you could use one of the RL
methods that we've covered.

214
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:52,880
Or you could use
a planning method.

215
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:55,450
And we'll talk about a couple
planning methods today.

216
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:59,020

217
00:09:59,020 --> 00:09:59,520
Cool.

218
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:00,440
So now, I have a
question for you.

219
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:02,340
So here's a sketch
of an algorithm.

220
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:05,060
Does anyone have thoughts
on what might go wrong

221
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:07,520
if you follow this sketch?

222
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:08,020
Yeah.

223
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:12,460
So the idea there was that
your simulator might not always

224
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:13,120
be accurate.

225
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:16,140
And so similar to what we saw
in reward modeling and also

226
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:19,500
arguably what we saw when
training Q-functions,

227
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,640
you might exploit
those inaccuracies.

228
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:23,360
Those inaccuracies
might compound.

229
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:26,780
And then what you've learned
in your learned simulator

230
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:29,780
might not transfer
to the real scenario

231
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:33,580
that you're trying to
learn a good policy in.

232
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:36,440
So there's also this
data coverage issue.

233
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:38,540
So similar to what
we've seen when

234
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:40,832
training Q-functions
and other things,

235
00:10:40,832 --> 00:10:45,300
if this pi 0, if
this initial policy,

236
00:10:45,300 --> 00:10:47,910
is different from the policy
that you're ultimately learning,

237
00:10:47,910 --> 00:10:49,577
then you're going to
have this mismatch.

238
00:10:49,577 --> 00:10:51,610
And you might not
have coverage--

239
00:10:51,610 --> 00:10:54,810
your model might not be
accurate for the states that

240
00:10:54,810 --> 00:10:58,210
are visited by the policy
that you're learning.

241
00:10:58,210 --> 00:11:04,930
So this is mostly basically
what's on this slide exactly.

242
00:11:04,930 --> 00:11:07,450
So if you have
some environment--

243
00:11:07,450 --> 00:11:09,930
maybe you want to
navigate a maze--

244
00:11:09,930 --> 00:11:13,878
then you might collect some
data set from some policy.

245
00:11:13,878 --> 00:11:16,170
Maybe the policy does something
like this yellow arrow.

246
00:11:16,170 --> 00:11:18,850
This is your data set.

247
00:11:18,850 --> 00:11:21,775
But this data set doesn't
cover the whole maze.

248
00:11:21,775 --> 00:11:24,150
And so then if you wanted to
go to this part of the maze,

249
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:25,970
for example, or this
part of the maze,

250
00:11:25,970 --> 00:11:29,730
then your model probably
wouldn't be very accurate

251
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:32,590
about the mazes in those
parts of the environment.

252
00:11:32,590 --> 00:11:35,290
And so then you wouldn't be able
to learn a policy for reaching

253
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:38,210
those parts of the maze.

254
00:11:38,210 --> 00:11:40,890
And then for the second
point, you might not always

255
00:11:40,890 --> 00:11:42,030
have an accurate simulator.

256
00:11:42,030 --> 00:11:45,760
So here's an example of
a video generated by Sora

257
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:47,320
with the prompt,
a humanoid robot

258
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:50,100
standing near a table with a
red, green, and blue cubes on it

259
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:52,600
performs a cube stacking
task with red on the bottom

260
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,200
and blue on the top.

261
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:57,400
And you can see that the
video generation does

262
00:11:57,400 --> 00:11:59,300
some creative
things with physics,

263
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:02,920
where it kind of changes
the colors of the block

264
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:06,560
and synthesizes blocks
out of thin air.

265
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:09,440
I would love for my robot to be
able to do something like this.

266
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:10,880
Seems very useful.

267
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:14,720
But this is not modeling
what robots can actually

268
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:17,280
do in reality.

269
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:19,360
And to this point,
when you're actually

270
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:21,840
trying to develop
this kind of model,

271
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:25,160
oftentimes you might
need to make choices

272
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:26,660
that are dependent
on the domain,

273
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:28,702
like if you're trying to
train a video prediction

274
00:12:28,702 --> 00:12:33,640
model versus training a
model of low-level physics

275
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:36,422
with friction or training a
model of the stock market.

276
00:12:36,422 --> 00:12:37,880
You might have some
choices to make

277
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:40,040
in terms of what are
the inputs to that model

278
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:41,498
and how are you
training it, what's

279
00:12:41,498 --> 00:12:43,440
the architecture, and so forth.

280
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:45,440
And some domains are
going to be much easier

281
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:48,520
to simulate than others.

282
00:12:48,520 --> 00:12:51,000
And then lastly,
for this last part,

283
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,520
even if you have a
really good simulator,

284
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:59,080
this doesn't mean that it'll
be easy to get a good policy

285
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:03,100
or to get actions that
are accurate as a result.

286
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:07,360
You still have to do the RL
process using the simulator.

287
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:10,560
And you need to account for
inaccuracies in the model

288
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:12,200
during this process.

289
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:15,620
Because usually, your
model is not perfect.

290
00:13:15,620 --> 00:13:19,120
You don't have a perfect
simulator of the world.

291
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:22,280
That's the key idea
behind model-based RL.

292
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,920
Now, let's talk a
little briefly about

293
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:25,990
how to learn a dynamics model.

294
00:13:25,990 --> 00:13:27,740
We don't have too much
time to cover this.

295
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:28,900
So we'll just talk
about it briefly.

296
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:30,720
And then we'll spend
most of the time

297
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:35,120
on how to use a dynamics model.

298
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:39,440
So in terms of learning a good
simulator or a good dynamics

299
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:43,030
model, there are some scenarios
where you might know it already.

300
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:46,670
And you could just use it.

301
00:13:46,670 --> 00:13:50,152
And then you're in great shape.

302
00:13:50,152 --> 00:13:52,110
There's also scenarios
where you might actually

303
00:13:52,110 --> 00:13:55,270
know most of the
model, like if you

304
00:13:55,270 --> 00:14:00,590
are in a simplified
robotic control setting

305
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:02,927
where you're trying to
push an object on a table.

306
00:14:02,927 --> 00:14:04,010
Maybe you know most of it.

307
00:14:04,010 --> 00:14:07,190
But you don't know the friction
coefficient of the table.

308
00:14:07,190 --> 00:14:12,990
Or in driving, maybe you
have a pretty good model

309
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:16,250
of how the car is
going to accelerate,

310
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:22,830
how it will move forward
with a given steering command

311
00:14:22,830 --> 00:14:24,170
and a gearing a given thing.

312
00:14:24,170 --> 00:14:27,630
And you just need to model
some additional things.

313
00:14:27,630 --> 00:14:29,470
In those cases,
you might actually

314
00:14:29,470 --> 00:14:32,950
be able to leverage knowledge
that you know about the world

315
00:14:32,950 --> 00:14:36,270
and then fit those unknown
parameters using the data.

316
00:14:36,270 --> 00:14:38,950
This is actually, I think,
one of the cooler ways

317
00:14:38,950 --> 00:14:41,870
to try to incorporate
domain-specific knowledge

318
00:14:41,870 --> 00:14:45,790
into reinforcement
learning algorithms.

319
00:14:45,790 --> 00:14:48,910
And then in almost all
scenarios in practice,

320
00:14:48,910 --> 00:14:52,430
you don't really know the model.

321
00:14:52,430 --> 00:14:55,710
And this might be because
you can't-- for example,

322
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:58,610
if you're trying to
manipulate some objects,

323
00:14:58,610 --> 00:15:00,850
maybe you kind of know
roughly the laws of physics.

324
00:15:00,850 --> 00:15:02,910
But you don't know
the vast majority

325
00:15:02,910 --> 00:15:05,422
of how an object is going
to behave because it has

326
00:15:05,422 --> 00:15:06,630
lots of different properties.

327
00:15:06,630 --> 00:15:08,663
And there's many
different objects.

328
00:15:08,663 --> 00:15:11,330
Or you don't know how people are
going to interact in the world.

329
00:15:11,330 --> 00:15:13,550
Or other drivers are going
to behave in the world

330
00:15:13,550 --> 00:15:15,230
and so forth.

331
00:15:15,230 --> 00:15:18,310
And in these scenarios,
the typical practice

332
00:15:18,310 --> 00:15:21,070
is to learn the
model from scratch

333
00:15:21,070 --> 00:15:25,290
with a neural network or some
other function approximator.

334
00:15:25,290 --> 00:15:28,470

335
00:15:28,470 --> 00:15:32,970
Now, there are a couple
options here as well.

336
00:15:32,970 --> 00:15:38,630
So if you are using image
observations, for example,

337
00:15:38,630 --> 00:15:42,245
and you're trying to predict--

338
00:15:42,245 --> 00:15:47,958
if your state space, for
example, is an image of--

339
00:15:47,958 --> 00:15:48,500
I don't know.

340
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:51,540
Maybe this is like you have a.

341
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:55,540
Car and there's other cars here.

342
00:15:55,540 --> 00:16:00,820
And you're trying to predict
in video where those cars will

343
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:02,920
be moving in the future.

344
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:06,292
And maybe there's, I don't
know, some trash on the ground

345
00:16:06,292 --> 00:16:07,250
or something like that.

346
00:16:07,250 --> 00:16:13,220

347
00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:17,140
I'll refer to this
image as st. One option

348
00:16:17,140 --> 00:16:23,340
is to generate
entire future video,

349
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:26,320
like the videos that we
saw on the previous slide.

350
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:32,740
This would be
conditioned on actions.

351
00:16:32,740 --> 00:16:36,220
But this might be
fairly expensive to do.

352
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:38,690
Because if you have
to basically be

353
00:16:38,690 --> 00:16:41,770
predicting this entire
video over time,

354
00:16:41,770 --> 00:16:42,990
that's a pretty large thing.

355
00:16:42,990 --> 00:16:45,130
And you have to model
all the different details

356
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:50,010
of how all of the pixels are
going to change in the future.

357
00:16:50,010 --> 00:16:52,970
And so one alternative
option that is sometimes

358
00:16:52,970 --> 00:16:56,610
used to learn dynamics
models is instead

359
00:16:56,610 --> 00:16:59,370
of modeling in the
image space, you

360
00:16:59,370 --> 00:17:04,369
could instead learn some lower
dimensional representation

361
00:17:04,369 --> 00:17:07,170
and then model in that
representation space.

362
00:17:07,170 --> 00:17:11,750
So you, for example,
for a given state,

363
00:17:11,750 --> 00:17:14,170
you could learn
some sort of encoder

364
00:17:14,170 --> 00:17:18,250
that maps this to a lower
dimensional representation

365
00:17:18,250 --> 00:17:18,750
space.

366
00:17:18,750 --> 00:17:22,010
Maybe we'll refer to this as zt.

367
00:17:22,010 --> 00:17:24,970
And then instead of learning
a model in the original image

368
00:17:24,970 --> 00:17:30,130
space, we could then try to
learn a model that predicts

369
00:17:30,130 --> 00:17:33,510
future zt's, like zt plus 1.

370
00:17:33,510 --> 00:17:38,410

371
00:17:38,410 --> 00:17:40,690
This can be a good
alternative in scenarios

372
00:17:40,690 --> 00:17:43,290
where it seems like it'll
be very expensive to learn

373
00:17:43,290 --> 00:17:44,300
a full model.

374
00:17:44,300 --> 00:17:46,050
It also has its own
challenges because you

375
00:17:46,050 --> 00:17:48,330
need to figure out how to
get this representation

376
00:17:48,330 --> 00:17:49,770
in the first place.

377
00:17:49,770 --> 00:17:52,570
And if you have a good way
to get good representations,

378
00:17:52,570 --> 00:17:55,250
it can be a good choice.

379
00:17:55,250 --> 00:17:57,650
So the question was we are
going to be losing information

380
00:17:57,650 --> 00:17:58,990
by going to a lower dimension.

381
00:17:58,990 --> 00:18:03,130
And so, is the main reason
to do this in terms of cost?

382
00:18:03,130 --> 00:18:03,950
Yeah, and exactly.

383
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:05,575
So the main reason
to do this is to try

384
00:18:05,575 --> 00:18:08,130
to reduce some of the
computational costs associated

385
00:18:08,130 --> 00:18:12,250
with this because you might
be losing information.

386
00:18:12,250 --> 00:18:13,770
And the key idea
is that there is

387
00:18:13,770 --> 00:18:17,650
some parts of this observation
that doesn't matter, perhaps

388
00:18:17,650 --> 00:18:20,730
like the exact
texture of the road,

389
00:18:20,730 --> 00:18:24,490
whether it's really dark black
or maybe a little bit worn down.

390
00:18:24,490 --> 00:18:25,990
In masonries, that
might not matter.

391
00:18:25,990 --> 00:18:27,865
And so if you can learn
a representation that

392
00:18:27,865 --> 00:18:30,570
discards that information
that's irrelevant,

393
00:18:30,570 --> 00:18:33,370
then you don't have to spend
so much compute modeling

394
00:18:33,370 --> 00:18:35,977
all those details
that don't matter.

395
00:18:35,977 --> 00:18:37,560
The other thing that
I'll mention here

396
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:41,385
is that oftentimes when you're
trying to learn these models,

397
00:18:41,385 --> 00:18:43,760
typically with supervised
learning or generative modeling

398
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:47,880
techniques, you often will also
need to learn a reward model as

399
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:48,777
well.

400
00:18:48,777 --> 00:18:50,360
And the reason for
this is that if you

401
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:54,120
want to be using your
model to generate data

402
00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:56,760
or to see into the
future, you need

403
00:18:56,760 --> 00:18:59,020
to see into the future
not just the states

404
00:18:59,020 --> 00:19:04,760
but also how those predicted
states will lead to rewards.

405
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:06,840
And so we'll talk
about what that looks

406
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:10,500
like on the next couple slides.

407
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:12,000
The question is,
does model learning

408
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,760
have to happen end to end?

409
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:19,240
The way that it works is we
are optimizing for our ability

410
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:22,640
to generate future states.

411
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:25,960
And there are
actually pros and cons

412
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:28,600
that we'll talk about with
regard to that objective.

413
00:19:28,600 --> 00:19:32,660
But one way to think
about it is like,

414
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:35,710
how well can I model
the distribution

415
00:19:35,710 --> 00:19:40,922
of P st plus 1 given st, at.

416
00:19:40,922 --> 00:19:42,630
Another way to think
about it, especially

417
00:19:42,630 --> 00:19:44,750
if you have more
deterministic dynamics,

418
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:52,030
is, how well can I
minimize the error

419
00:19:52,030 --> 00:20:02,830
between my predicted st plus
1 and the actual st plus 1?

420
00:20:02,830 --> 00:20:05,580
So how do we go about
using a dynamics model?

421
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:09,734

422
00:20:09,734 --> 00:20:11,510
The first approach
that we'll talk about

423
00:20:11,510 --> 00:20:13,593
is we'll actually start
by talking about planning.

424
00:20:13,593 --> 00:20:16,010
And so this is actually going
to be a little bit different

425
00:20:16,010 --> 00:20:18,270
than simply running
reinforcement learning

426
00:20:18,270 --> 00:20:21,910
inside your learn simulator.

427
00:20:21,910 --> 00:20:26,590
And the way that
this could work is

428
00:20:26,590 --> 00:20:30,230
you can think about
your MDP, where

429
00:20:30,230 --> 00:20:33,270
you have your current
state that you're in.

430
00:20:33,270 --> 00:20:36,030
And then you take some action.

431
00:20:36,030 --> 00:20:41,790
And then this affects
your next action.

432
00:20:41,790 --> 00:20:44,310
And then as a result
of that, you'll

433
00:20:44,310 --> 00:20:48,270
also get some future reward.

434
00:20:48,270 --> 00:20:51,982
And then this
process will repeat.

435
00:20:51,982 --> 00:20:55,830
You'll take another
action that influences

436
00:20:55,830 --> 00:21:01,010
the state after that, which will
yield some additional reward.

437
00:21:01,010 --> 00:21:03,670
This reward might also
depend on the action

438
00:21:03,670 --> 00:21:06,350
that you took as well.

439
00:21:06,350 --> 00:21:11,670
Now, in some of the
past reinforcement

440
00:21:11,670 --> 00:21:13,403
learning works that
we talked about,

441
00:21:13,403 --> 00:21:15,070
we kind of assumed
that we didn't really

442
00:21:15,070 --> 00:21:16,970
know the relationship.

443
00:21:16,970 --> 00:21:21,270
We kind of don't know how the
actions affect future states.

444
00:21:21,270 --> 00:21:24,110
And so we have to figure
out how to optimize rewards

445
00:21:24,110 --> 00:21:28,550
without using direct gradients.

446
00:21:28,550 --> 00:21:35,140
But if we can actually
predict what a future state is

447
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:37,760
and what a reward is
based off of that,

448
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:40,980
if we train a reward model
and a dynamics model,

449
00:21:40,980 --> 00:21:42,500
then we could
actually just directly

450
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:48,020
backpropagate from
rewards into actions.

451
00:21:48,020 --> 00:21:53,140
And so we could potentially
directly pass gradients

452
00:21:53,140 --> 00:21:58,860
from your rewards
into your actions.

453
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:01,060
And what this would look
like is your objective

454
00:22:01,060 --> 00:22:10,660
would be to maximize either
just the current action or maybe

455
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:20,780
the next sequence of actions
for the sum of future rewards.

456
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:24,600
Maybe we'll do this t
prime for some horizon.

457
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:29,540
So maybe we're optimizing
for rewards from time t

458
00:22:29,540 --> 00:22:36,580
to time t plus H.

459
00:22:36,580 --> 00:22:39,460
What this would
look specifically--

460
00:22:39,460 --> 00:22:42,200
I guess we can show it
on the slides as well.

461
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:44,820
So we're trying to maximize
the sum of rewards.

462
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:49,860
And we often refer to
this kind of problem

463
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:53,700
as looking into the future is
planning a sequence of actions

464
00:22:53,700 --> 00:22:56,060
that will maximize rewards.

465
00:22:56,060 --> 00:23:00,700
And so if we have some model
of how we predict future states

466
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:02,780
and actions and
rewards and if we're

467
00:23:02,780 --> 00:23:05,060
modeling that with
a neural network,

468
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:08,580
then we can also compute the
gradients of that neural network

469
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:15,660
to backpropagate rewards
into actions as following.

470
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:20,780
And so then, specifically,
what this would look like is--

471
00:23:20,780 --> 00:23:24,030
well, first, you would
collect some data.

472
00:23:24,030 --> 00:23:27,807

473
00:23:27,807 --> 00:23:29,140
Then you would train your model.

474
00:23:29,140 --> 00:23:35,610

475
00:23:35,610 --> 00:23:44,330
Maybe that's to minimize
f of s, a minus the--

476
00:23:44,330 --> 00:23:45,750
this is your
predicted next state.

477
00:23:45,750 --> 00:23:48,490
This is your true next state.

478
00:23:48,490 --> 00:23:50,490
And then once you
have this model,

479
00:23:50,490 --> 00:23:56,890
you could then find a
set of actions that is--

480
00:23:56,890 --> 00:23:59,810
actually, just to be explicit
for this optimization,

481
00:23:59,810 --> 00:24:04,530
we're minimizing the
parameters of our model.

482
00:24:04,530 --> 00:24:09,560
And then the third step is to
try to find a set of actions.

483
00:24:09,560 --> 00:24:12,850

484
00:24:12,850 --> 00:24:19,930
So this would be a hat t
to t plus H. This is like,

485
00:24:19,930 --> 00:24:23,130
what is the course of actions
that will lead to good rewards?

486
00:24:23,130 --> 00:24:36,490
And this would be the argmax
of the sum of rewards--

487
00:24:36,490 --> 00:24:42,280

488
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:44,120
sum of t.

489
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:45,060
This is over--

490
00:24:45,060 --> 00:24:57,120

491
00:24:57,120 --> 00:24:59,450
Running gradient descent on
actions is a little bit--

492
00:24:59,450 --> 00:25:01,200
backpropagation to
actions is a little bit

493
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:04,560
different from the typical
backprop that we look at.

494
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:06,640
So oftentimes, when
we run backprop,

495
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:09,320
we're doing it with regard to
parameters of a neural network.

496
00:25:09,320 --> 00:25:11,800
Here, we're actually
backpropagating

497
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:14,400
into these actions right here.

498
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:17,200
And so specifically, what
this kind of third step

499
00:25:17,200 --> 00:25:25,596
would look like is you will
need to basically initialize--

500
00:25:25,596 --> 00:25:28,312

501
00:25:28,312 --> 00:25:30,520
just like you randomly
initialize your neural network

502
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:33,330
parameters, you'll need to
initialize your actions.

503
00:25:33,330 --> 00:25:36,480

504
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:40,840
Then you need to
compute your gradient.

505
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:53,200
So this would be the gradient of
the sum of rewards with respect

506
00:25:53,200 --> 00:25:58,440
to the actions that you take.

507
00:25:58,440 --> 00:26:01,040
And to compute
this gradient, you

508
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:04,000
can think of it essentially--
like if you use the chain rule,

509
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:09,320
the gradient will be the
sum of rewards with respect

510
00:26:09,320 --> 00:26:11,590
to the states.

511
00:26:11,590 --> 00:26:14,440

512
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:16,750
So that would just be based
off of your reward model.

513
00:26:16,750 --> 00:26:19,305

514
00:26:19,305 --> 00:26:20,680
Then if you apply
the chain rule,

515
00:26:20,680 --> 00:26:22,780
you could then
have a second term

516
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:27,870
that's d states d
actions to actually get

517
00:26:27,870 --> 00:26:30,360
the gradient of the rewards
with respect to the actions.

518
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:37,030

519
00:26:37,030 --> 00:26:39,290
And then once you
have your gradient,

520
00:26:39,290 --> 00:26:43,470
then you could use it
to update your actions

521
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:44,830
with gradient descent.

522
00:26:44,830 --> 00:26:51,190
And so then maybe this
is your initial a hat

523
00:26:51,190 --> 00:26:55,110
a hat t to t plus
H is going to be

524
00:26:55,110 --> 00:27:06,270
your previous a hat minus alpha
times this gradient right here.

525
00:27:06,270 --> 00:27:08,530
I'm going to put
this separately.

526
00:27:08,530 --> 00:27:11,935

527
00:27:11,935 --> 00:27:12,810
Yeah, great question.

528
00:27:12,810 --> 00:27:14,872
Actions would be
continuous in this setting.

529
00:27:14,872 --> 00:27:16,830
We'll also look at
scenarios where we can do it

530
00:27:16,830 --> 00:27:18,602
with discrete actions as well.

531
00:27:18,602 --> 00:27:20,310
We're assuming rewards
are deterministic.

532
00:27:20,310 --> 00:27:21,685
But we've actually
always assumed

533
00:27:21,685 --> 00:27:23,660
rewards are deterministic.

534
00:27:23,660 --> 00:27:27,060
And you can consider any
sort of non-determinism

535
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:28,900
being encapsulated
by the dynamics

536
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:31,060
and in the state variable.

537
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:31,720
Correct, yeah.

538
00:27:31,720 --> 00:27:33,620
So here, we have
a dynamics model.

539
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:35,740
And I guess I didn't
explicitly write this.

540
00:27:35,740 --> 00:27:42,900
But you would also be
trading some reward function.

541
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:46,580
Maybe this is like
r psi for example.

542
00:27:46,580 --> 00:27:49,740
And this would be using some of
the reward learning techniques

543
00:27:49,740 --> 00:27:50,760
that we've talked about.

544
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:53,940
There's also some scenarios
where you might know the reward.

545
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:57,260
So it might be, for example,
like forward velocity if you're

546
00:27:57,260 --> 00:28:00,720
trying to learn how to walk
or some distance to some goal,

547
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:01,600
that sort of thing.

548
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:05,260
And in those cases, you don't
need to learn a reward function.

549
00:28:05,260 --> 00:28:08,800
So the question is, if your
dynamics model is stochastic,

550
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:10,840
how do you backprop through it?

551
00:28:10,840 --> 00:28:17,300

552
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:21,020
It kind of depends on how you're
modeling the stochasticity.

553
00:28:21,020 --> 00:28:24,320
So if you're using a
Gaussian, for example,

554
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:26,820
there are certain tricks for
differentiating through samples

555
00:28:26,820 --> 00:28:30,340
from Gaussian, whereas if you're
using something like a diffusion

556
00:28:30,340 --> 00:28:33,620
model, it would look
a bit different.

557
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:37,300
So it is kind of
model dependent.

558
00:28:37,300 --> 00:28:44,460
And so far, we're talking
about deterministic models.

559
00:28:44,460 --> 00:28:47,140
So the question is, will
this start to exploit errors

560
00:28:47,140 --> 00:28:48,248
in the reward?

561
00:28:48,248 --> 00:28:50,540
So this will start to exploit
both errors in the reward

562
00:28:50,540 --> 00:28:52,360
but also errors in
the dynamics model.

563
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:56,380
So if your dynamics
model is inaccurate

564
00:28:56,380 --> 00:28:58,040
or if your reward
model is inaccurate--

565
00:28:58,040 --> 00:28:59,820
if either one of
them is inaccurate,

566
00:28:59,820 --> 00:29:03,260
then you might get actions
that aren't actually

567
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:06,340
going to take good
actions in the world.

568
00:29:06,340 --> 00:29:09,180
And that's a problem.

569
00:29:09,180 --> 00:29:11,875
And so we'll talk
about that in a second.

570
00:29:11,875 --> 00:29:13,000
So this is a good question.

571
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:16,020
So I guess instead
of repeating it,

572
00:29:16,020 --> 00:29:18,720
I think what would
be helpful is--

573
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:20,470
we aren't actually
learning a policy here.

574
00:29:20,470 --> 00:29:22,530
And that's essentially
what you're pointing out.

575
00:29:22,530 --> 00:29:32,530
And this is like becoming
our "policy," quote unquote.

576
00:29:32,530 --> 00:29:33,930
This is like our behavior.

577
00:29:33,930 --> 00:29:36,798

578
00:29:36,798 --> 00:29:38,090
Do I have a good term for this?

579
00:29:38,090 --> 00:29:38,510
I guess.

580
00:29:38,510 --> 00:29:39,052
I mean, yeah.

581
00:29:39,052 --> 00:29:44,850
This is sort of the
"policy," in quotes.

582
00:29:44,850 --> 00:29:47,870
And this is what
we call planning,

583
00:29:47,870 --> 00:29:50,090
where at every
single state, we're

584
00:29:50,090 --> 00:29:53,295
actually going to be planning
forward and thinking ahead.

585
00:29:53,295 --> 00:29:55,170
What are the actions I
should consider that I

586
00:29:55,170 --> 00:29:58,610
think will lead to good reward?

587
00:29:58,610 --> 00:30:01,010
And so we'll actually be doing
this optimization process,

588
00:30:01,010 --> 00:30:06,850
every single state when
we're doing planning.

589
00:30:06,850 --> 00:30:09,890
So on the slide,
we're going to be

590
00:30:09,890 --> 00:30:12,610
running some policy
to collect data,

591
00:30:12,610 --> 00:30:17,090
learning a model to try to
be able to accurately model

592
00:30:17,090 --> 00:30:20,410
the dynamics, potentially also
learning a reward function as

593
00:30:20,410 --> 00:30:22,370
well.

594
00:30:22,370 --> 00:30:23,970
And then we'll be
backpropagating

595
00:30:23,970 --> 00:30:27,490
through the reward
and the dynamics

596
00:30:27,490 --> 00:30:31,770
in order to choose actions.

597
00:30:31,770 --> 00:30:33,850
And then once we
choose those actions,

598
00:30:33,850 --> 00:30:37,730
we'll be then be executing these
actions in the environment.

599
00:30:37,730 --> 00:30:40,570
And then after we
execute those actions,

600
00:30:40,570 --> 00:30:44,290
we'll then be rerunning
this planning process again.

601
00:30:44,290 --> 00:30:48,290
So in particular, I guess maybe
to explicitly write that out,

602
00:30:48,290 --> 00:30:50,970
we'll have a step 4 here.

603
00:30:50,970 --> 00:31:00,450
That is to actually execute
a hat t to t plus H.

604
00:31:00,450 --> 00:31:08,770
And then you will then move
on back to step 3 after you've

605
00:31:08,770 --> 00:31:12,250
executed these H actions.

606
00:31:12,250 --> 00:31:17,070
So the question is, we're only
planning over a horizon of H.

607
00:31:17,070 --> 00:31:19,490
And what if we have
an infinite horizon?

608
00:31:19,490 --> 00:31:23,760

609
00:31:23,760 --> 00:31:27,020
In principle, the math--

610
00:31:27,020 --> 00:31:30,370
well, it's a good question.

611
00:31:30,370 --> 00:31:33,040

612
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:35,017
So we'll talk a
little bit about this.

613
00:31:35,017 --> 00:31:37,600
For now, we're going to be only
considering this short horizon

614
00:31:37,600 --> 00:31:40,000
planning.

615
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,937
And in eight-ish
slides or something,

616
00:31:42,937 --> 00:31:44,520
I'll talk a little
bit more about what

617
00:31:44,520 --> 00:31:46,478
happens if you want to
do infinite or very long

618
00:31:46,478 --> 00:31:47,760
horizon things.

619
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:50,540
And we'll also talk about some
of the shortcomings of this.

620
00:31:50,540 --> 00:31:56,800

621
00:31:56,800 --> 00:32:01,080
Now, so far, we talked
about gradient descent

622
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:03,680
for optimizing actions.

623
00:32:03,680 --> 00:32:06,120
And this is actually
not the only way

624
00:32:06,120 --> 00:32:07,660
that you can do optimization.

625
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:09,520
So it's probably the
optimization technique

626
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:12,000
that you're most familiar
with because we use it all

627
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:13,560
the time for neural networks.

628
00:32:13,560 --> 00:32:17,510
But there's other ways that
we could optimize a function,

629
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:21,750
specifically when we're trying
to optimize the sum of rewards.

630
00:32:21,750 --> 00:32:25,390
And if we think about
gradient-based optimization,

631
00:32:25,390 --> 00:32:28,770
typically we have something that
we're trying to optimize over.

632
00:32:28,770 --> 00:32:32,650
In this case, we're trying to
be optimizing over actions.

633
00:32:32,650 --> 00:32:35,350
And the thing that
we're trying to optimize

634
00:32:35,350 --> 00:32:40,240
is the sum of rewards maybe.

635
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:44,055

636
00:32:44,055 --> 00:32:45,930
Actually, also, there's
a small mistake here.

637
00:32:45,930 --> 00:32:48,097
This should be a plus sign
because we're maximizing,

638
00:32:48,097 --> 00:32:50,327
not minimizing.

639
00:32:50,327 --> 00:32:52,910
So the way that gradient descent
works is you kind of randomly

640
00:32:52,910 --> 00:32:56,070
initialize some action,
like right here.

641
00:32:56,070 --> 00:32:58,930
And then you evaluate the
gradient at that position.

642
00:32:58,930 --> 00:33:01,030
So maybe you find
that the gradient

643
00:33:01,030 --> 00:33:04,790
is like pointed this way.

644
00:33:04,790 --> 00:33:09,470
And then you then say, OK, I
should move probably over here.

645
00:33:09,470 --> 00:33:11,505
And then maybe you sample this.

646
00:33:11,505 --> 00:33:14,130
You choose this action because
that's kind of a step size away.

647
00:33:14,130 --> 00:33:16,110
You evaluate the gradient there.

648
00:33:16,110 --> 00:33:19,870
And you find that the gradient
maybe looks something like this.

649
00:33:19,870 --> 00:33:21,950
And then you say, OK,
maybe I should move further

650
00:33:21,950 --> 00:33:23,325
to the left but
a little bit less

651
00:33:23,325 --> 00:33:25,710
because the slope is less steep.

652
00:33:25,710 --> 00:33:28,910
And then you get maybe
something that looks like this.

653
00:33:28,910 --> 00:33:31,330
And you're like, OK,
the gradient is 0.

654
00:33:31,330 --> 00:33:34,890
And so maybe I won't be
sampling any further actions.

655
00:33:34,890 --> 00:33:38,590
And then your final choice
after running gradient descent

656
00:33:38,590 --> 00:33:41,990
is this value right here.

657
00:33:41,990 --> 00:33:46,990
Now, an alternative way to try
to do a kind of optimization

658
00:33:46,990 --> 00:33:50,650
is instead what I'll call
sampling-based optimization.

659
00:33:50,650 --> 00:33:54,270
This is also called
gradient-free optimization.

660
00:33:54,270 --> 00:33:58,030
And the way that this works is
if you have some set of actions

661
00:33:58,030 --> 00:34:04,350
and some sum of rewards, instead
of just starting from one place

662
00:34:04,350 --> 00:34:06,190
and evaluating the
gradient, we're

663
00:34:06,190 --> 00:34:08,510
actually not going to
use the gradients at all.

664
00:34:08,510 --> 00:34:10,630
And we'll just sample
a bunch of actions

665
00:34:10,630 --> 00:34:13,060
and see what the values are.

666
00:34:13,060 --> 00:34:15,900
So maybe we'll, for example,
a sample like this action.

667
00:34:15,900 --> 00:34:22,620
We'll sample this action maybe
sample this one, maybe sample

668
00:34:22,620 --> 00:34:26,219
this one, and so forth.

669
00:34:26,219 --> 00:34:31,060
And the first version of
a sampling-based method

670
00:34:31,060 --> 00:34:35,060
could just be to sample a
bunch of things in parallel

671
00:34:35,060 --> 00:34:38,139
and then pick the best one.

672
00:34:38,139 --> 00:34:41,940
This is basically just
called random sampling.

673
00:34:41,940 --> 00:34:44,739
Sometimes, it
actually works better

674
00:34:44,739 --> 00:34:46,003
than you think it might do.

675
00:34:46,003 --> 00:34:47,420
And if we were to
do that, then we

676
00:34:47,420 --> 00:34:50,239
would say that, OK, this
action has the highest.

677
00:34:50,239 --> 00:34:54,940
And so maybe I will return
the corresponding action

678
00:34:54,940 --> 00:34:57,085
for this value.

679
00:34:57,085 --> 00:34:59,460
But you can also do something
a bit better than that even

680
00:34:59,460 --> 00:35:01,595
without gradient
optimization, which

681
00:35:01,595 --> 00:35:03,220
is that you could
look at these samples

682
00:35:03,220 --> 00:35:05,644
and say, OK, it looks like--

683
00:35:05,644 --> 00:35:09,700
maybe we have one additional
sample thing that we've--

684
00:35:09,700 --> 00:35:13,250
maybe we've also sampled
something like this.

685
00:35:13,250 --> 00:35:15,650
And in that case, one
thing that we could do

686
00:35:15,650 --> 00:35:19,050
is instead of just
considering this one

687
00:35:19,050 --> 00:35:21,570
and taking the best is we could
say, maybe we should sample

688
00:35:21,570 --> 00:35:26,450
more over here, because these
are the ones that were the best.

689
00:35:26,450 --> 00:35:31,490
And so we could
basically try to sample

690
00:35:31,490 --> 00:35:32,990
more from the things
that were good.

691
00:35:32,990 --> 00:35:34,448
So we could take
the ones that were

692
00:35:34,448 --> 00:35:37,330
the best fit, some kind of
distribution over those,

693
00:35:37,330 --> 00:35:39,788
like a simple distribution,
like a Gaussian.

694
00:35:39,788 --> 00:35:40,330
Say that, OK.

695
00:35:40,330 --> 00:35:42,080
These were the actions
that were the best.

696
00:35:42,080 --> 00:35:44,250
So maybe we could fit
some Gaussian or sample

697
00:35:44,250 --> 00:35:47,490
more from the actions
here and then do

698
00:35:47,490 --> 00:35:51,290
another round of sampling from
that new distribution, where

699
00:35:51,290 --> 00:35:53,790
now you're going to be sampling
more from this distribution.

700
00:35:53,790 --> 00:35:57,010
You find that when you sample
for a second round, maybe

701
00:35:57,010 --> 00:36:01,890
you get samples
that look like this.

702
00:36:01,890 --> 00:36:03,570
And then that allows
you to hone in

703
00:36:03,570 --> 00:36:07,110
on what a good action might be.

704
00:36:07,110 --> 00:36:11,170

705
00:36:11,170 --> 00:36:16,370
So on the slides,
instead of with actions

706
00:36:16,370 --> 00:36:19,450
and maximizing rewards, I did it
with parameters and minimizing

707
00:36:19,450 --> 00:36:20,690
some loss function.

708
00:36:20,690 --> 00:36:22,850
But all the ideas are the same.

709
00:36:22,850 --> 00:36:27,950
So for gradient descent,
we pick an initial value,

710
00:36:27,950 --> 00:36:30,910
evaluate the gradient, move in
the direction of the gradient,

711
00:36:30,910 --> 00:36:32,590
and repeat that process.

712
00:36:32,590 --> 00:36:36,930
There's some underlying
function that we don't know.

713
00:36:36,930 --> 00:36:40,930
And so we're trying to get to
the minimum of that function

714
00:36:40,930 --> 00:36:43,090
using these gradients.

715
00:36:43,090 --> 00:36:45,770
And likewise with sampling,
or another way to call this is

716
00:36:45,770 --> 00:36:49,010
a 0th order optimization, we're
instead going to be sampling

717
00:36:49,010 --> 00:36:55,090
points and trying to figure out
what a good value is just from

718
00:36:55,090 --> 00:36:56,010
sampling.

719
00:36:56,010 --> 00:36:57,490
And again, there
is some underlying

720
00:36:57,490 --> 00:36:59,630
curve here that we don't know.

721
00:36:59,630 --> 00:37:04,170
We can only evaluate samples or,
in the case of gradient descent,

722
00:37:04,170 --> 00:37:06,850
the gradient at those samples.

723
00:37:06,850 --> 00:37:09,440
One other nice thing about a
sampling-based optimization

724
00:37:09,440 --> 00:37:11,600
is that it means that
you don't need gradients.

725
00:37:11,600 --> 00:37:14,580
So it can apply to
discrete variables.

726
00:37:14,580 --> 00:37:18,240
It can also apply to
scenarios where you maybe

727
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:20,160
know the reward function.

728
00:37:20,160 --> 00:37:21,500
You have reward values.

729
00:37:21,500 --> 00:37:24,000
But you don't know what the
gradient of the reward function

730
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:24,500
is.

731
00:37:24,500 --> 00:37:29,980

732
00:37:29,980 --> 00:37:31,860
If it's a discrete
reward function,

733
00:37:31,860 --> 00:37:35,480
then getting the gradient
might be challenging.

734
00:37:35,480 --> 00:37:39,120
And then to formalize this
kind of iterative sampling

735
00:37:39,120 --> 00:37:41,000
process, where we're
trying to sample more

736
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:44,920
from the good actions,
one method for doing this

737
00:37:44,920 --> 00:37:46,920
is cross-entropy method.

738
00:37:46,920 --> 00:37:49,860
There's also a way to
do this with-- well,

739
00:37:49,860 --> 00:37:53,240
if you have discrete actions,
you could also just--

740
00:37:53,240 --> 00:37:55,620
well, there's somewhat
of a different process.

741
00:37:55,620 --> 00:37:57,480
But you could also just
use random sampling

742
00:37:57,480 --> 00:38:00,600
if your action space
is small enough.

743
00:38:00,600 --> 00:38:03,040
So what cross-entropy
method, not

744
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:04,880
to be confused with
the cross-entropy loss,

745
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:07,180
is you sample from some
initial distribution.

746
00:38:07,180 --> 00:38:10,000
This could be a
uniform distribution.

747
00:38:10,000 --> 00:38:12,830
Then rank the samples
according to their loss.

748
00:38:12,830 --> 00:38:16,080

749
00:38:16,080 --> 00:38:18,002
Like, this would
be the best one.

750
00:38:18,002 --> 00:38:20,460
This would be the second best,
the third best, fourth best,

751
00:38:20,460 --> 00:38:22,080
and so forth.

752
00:38:22,080 --> 00:38:25,320
And then take the best ones
like, these three ones.

753
00:38:25,320 --> 00:38:29,920
And then fit a distribution,
like a Gaussian distribution,

754
00:38:29,920 --> 00:38:33,000
to those best samples.

755
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:36,560
And then resample from
your distribution,

756
00:38:36,560 --> 00:38:39,920
rerank, and repeat this process.

757
00:38:39,920 --> 00:38:43,840
So it's basically like a very
simple sampling-based way

758
00:38:43,840 --> 00:38:47,480
to try to get, in this
case, good actions.

759
00:38:47,480 --> 00:38:50,000
It's like a general
optimization technique

760
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:53,820
that can be used for
any sort of objective.

761
00:38:53,820 --> 00:38:56,480

762
00:38:56,480 --> 00:38:56,980
Cool.

763
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:59,640
And then after this
process, eventually,

764
00:38:59,640 --> 00:39:01,380
after you run some
number of iterations,

765
00:39:01,380 --> 00:39:04,160
you'll return the
best sample that you

766
00:39:04,160 --> 00:39:06,800
found in the last iteration.

767
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:08,550
The question is, can
you also use sampling

768
00:39:08,550 --> 00:39:10,910
to approximate gradients?

769
00:39:10,910 --> 00:39:11,470
Yes.

770
00:39:11,470 --> 00:39:15,310
So there's something called
finite differences, where

771
00:39:15,310 --> 00:39:17,250
if you don't have a
way to get gradients,

772
00:39:17,250 --> 00:39:19,310
then you could try
to sample basically

773
00:39:19,310 --> 00:39:23,030
points very close together and
then use that to basically look

774
00:39:23,030 --> 00:39:27,230
at the difference between this
point and this point and use

775
00:39:27,230 --> 00:39:28,870
that to estimate the gradient.

776
00:39:28,870 --> 00:39:31,490
There's pros and cons to it.

777
00:39:31,490 --> 00:39:34,270
But there's definitely
a valid approach.

778
00:39:34,270 --> 00:39:34,910
Yeah.

779
00:39:34,910 --> 00:39:37,327
The question is, why do I put
the quotes around the policy

780
00:39:37,327 --> 00:39:38,430
here?

781
00:39:38,430 --> 00:39:42,610
The reason is that we're not
learning a parametric policy.

782
00:39:42,610 --> 00:39:45,990
We're not learning a neural
network to represent a policy.

783
00:39:45,990 --> 00:39:50,230
And there's actually
an optimization process

784
00:39:50,230 --> 00:39:52,950
underlying the policy here.

785
00:39:52,950 --> 00:39:57,670
And so typically, it's
a policy in the sense

786
00:39:57,670 --> 00:40:01,270
that it is mapping from a
state to an action or a state

787
00:40:01,270 --> 00:40:04,710
to actually multiple
future actions.

788
00:40:04,710 --> 00:40:09,125
But it's often not
referred to a policy

789
00:40:09,125 --> 00:40:10,500
to differentiate
it from policies

790
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:13,820
that are more like
neural network,

791
00:40:13,820 --> 00:40:16,740
like that are more compact.

792
00:40:16,740 --> 00:40:19,300
Well, you can also have
a linear function that's

793
00:40:19,300 --> 00:40:20,740
mapping states to actions.

794
00:40:20,740 --> 00:40:22,580
The main thing is that
it's a process that's

795
00:40:22,580 --> 00:40:24,480
like an iterative
optimization process

796
00:40:24,480 --> 00:40:27,140
rather than some sort of
closed form, like here's

797
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:28,730
an action for a state.

798
00:40:28,730 --> 00:40:30,980
So the question is, can we
talk a little bit about how

799
00:40:30,980 --> 00:40:32,700
this policy is stored?

800
00:40:32,700 --> 00:40:39,860
So the only thing that we
have learned at this point

801
00:40:39,860 --> 00:40:43,380
is a dynamics model and
potentially a reward function.

802
00:40:43,380 --> 00:40:45,580
And so the only thing
that we have stored

803
00:40:45,580 --> 00:40:49,100
is the parameters of the
model and the parameters

804
00:40:49,100 --> 00:40:51,740
of your reward model.

805
00:40:51,740 --> 00:40:56,140
And then when you're actually
trying to figure out what to do

806
00:40:56,140 --> 00:40:59,420
and what actions to take,
what policy to follow,

807
00:40:59,420 --> 00:41:01,420
we don't have a neural
network that's telling us

808
00:41:01,420 --> 00:41:02,295
what actions to take.

809
00:41:02,295 --> 00:41:04,880
Instead, every single time
step, you're thinking, OK,

810
00:41:04,880 --> 00:41:07,820
what's going to happen if
I do this, this, or this?

811
00:41:07,820 --> 00:41:09,980
And then choosing
actions that you think

812
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:15,060
are good as a result of that
kind of thinking process.

813
00:41:15,060 --> 00:41:17,920
You're using a lot more compute
as part of this process.

814
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:19,900
And you're doing
that in real time

815
00:41:19,900 --> 00:41:22,980
as you're trying to figure
out what actions to take.

816
00:41:22,980 --> 00:41:30,260
Yeah, we're not recording
what we're doing right now.

817
00:41:30,260 --> 00:41:34,420
So the question is, when we
sample actions, can some of them

818
00:41:34,420 --> 00:41:35,480
be out of distribution?

819
00:41:35,480 --> 00:41:40,700
So yeah, when you select the
distribution to sample from,

820
00:41:40,700 --> 00:41:42,900
your actions might be
out of distribution

821
00:41:42,900 --> 00:41:45,300
compared to the data that
the model was trained on.

822
00:41:45,300 --> 00:41:50,620
And we'll talk in the next
slide on how to deal with that.

823
00:41:50,620 --> 00:41:52,260
So the question
is, because we're

824
00:41:52,260 --> 00:41:54,220
doing so much
compute at test time,

825
00:41:54,220 --> 00:41:57,340
if you've finished computing
and by the time you finish

826
00:41:57,340 --> 00:41:59,003
computing, the
state has changed,

827
00:41:59,003 --> 00:42:00,170
how do you account for that?

828
00:42:00,170 --> 00:42:05,073

829
00:42:05,073 --> 00:42:06,990
I don't know if I have
a great answer for you.

830
00:42:06,990 --> 00:42:09,790
I think that, typically, when
people run this in practice,

831
00:42:09,790 --> 00:42:13,490
they just try to make that
compute process as fast

832
00:42:13,490 --> 00:42:18,710
as possible so that they don't
have to account for the change.

833
00:42:18,710 --> 00:42:25,330
I guess one thing you can do is
you're kind of planning actually

834
00:42:25,330 --> 00:42:26,750
a sequence of actions here.

835
00:42:26,750 --> 00:42:33,850
And so if you can estimate how
long that computation took--

836
00:42:33,850 --> 00:42:36,490
if it took like two time steps,
for example, then instead

837
00:42:36,490 --> 00:42:38,490
of starting by executing
at, you could actually

838
00:42:38,490 --> 00:42:42,330
start by executing at
plus 3 or at plus 2,

839
00:42:42,330 --> 00:42:45,410
depending on how
[? that ?] one works.

840
00:42:45,410 --> 00:42:47,690
And so that's kind of
accounting for the fact

841
00:42:47,690 --> 00:42:50,650
that time has moved forward.

842
00:42:50,650 --> 00:42:53,850
The downside there
is that at and at

843
00:42:53,850 --> 00:42:56,303
plus 1 might be different from
what was actually executed.

844
00:42:56,303 --> 00:42:57,970
And so you might be
in a different state

845
00:42:57,970 --> 00:43:00,130
from where you thought
you ended up in.

846
00:43:00,130 --> 00:43:02,860
And so that
introduces challenges.

847
00:43:02,860 --> 00:43:05,600
And it's actually somewhat
of an open research problem

848
00:43:05,600 --> 00:43:12,420
on how best to handle
that sort of thing,

849
00:43:12,420 --> 00:43:17,280
especially if you're basically
asynchronously like running

850
00:43:17,280 --> 00:43:18,040
this process.

851
00:43:18,040 --> 00:43:21,780
And also, the world
is evolving as well.

852
00:43:21,780 --> 00:43:27,760

853
00:43:27,760 --> 00:43:30,480
So going back to doing
this with gradient descent

854
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:34,280
versus with samples,
there's pros and cons

855
00:43:34,280 --> 00:43:36,020
to gradient-based
and sampling-based.

856
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:38,520
And this helps elucidate why
sampling-based actually can

857
00:43:38,520 --> 00:43:41,200
be a good choice for planning.

858
00:43:41,200 --> 00:43:44,960
So gradient descent
is very scalable even

859
00:43:44,960 --> 00:43:47,120
to very high dimensions.

860
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:50,492
And it works well, especially
in overparameterized regimes,

861
00:43:50,492 --> 00:43:52,200
when you have a big
neural network that's

862
00:43:52,200 --> 00:43:54,080
representing a function
that may actually

863
00:43:54,080 --> 00:43:57,840
have fewer free parameters.

864
00:43:57,840 --> 00:44:00,400
The downside is that it
requires a nice optimization

865
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:06,560
landscape where your gradients
are fairly smooth rather

866
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:09,480
than not smooth, I guess.

867
00:44:09,480 --> 00:44:11,583
With neural networks,
it ends up being--

868
00:44:11,583 --> 00:44:13,000
especially because
neural networks

869
00:44:13,000 --> 00:44:14,760
have high dimensional
parameter spaces

870
00:44:14,760 --> 00:44:17,440
and are overparameterized,
the pros

871
00:44:17,440 --> 00:44:20,277
end up heavily
outweighing the cons here.

872
00:44:20,277 --> 00:44:22,360
Some of the upsides with
sampling-based approaches

873
00:44:22,360 --> 00:44:24,800
is that it's really easy
to parallelize because you

874
00:44:24,800 --> 00:44:28,800
can actually estimate
multiple samples

875
00:44:28,800 --> 00:44:30,800
in parallel, whereas
gradient descent,

876
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:33,502
it inevitably has to be a
very sequential process, where

877
00:44:33,502 --> 00:44:35,960
you compute one gradient and
then apply it and then compute

878
00:44:35,960 --> 00:44:37,560
the next gradient and so forth.

879
00:44:37,560 --> 00:44:40,020
And this parallelization
can actually, in some cases,

880
00:44:40,020 --> 00:44:42,080
make it faster.

881
00:44:42,080 --> 00:44:45,320
It doesn't require gradients
if you don't have them.

882
00:44:45,320 --> 00:44:48,580
The downside is that it scales
poorly to high dimensions.

883
00:44:48,580 --> 00:44:50,780
Because if you have a really
high dimensional space,

884
00:44:50,780 --> 00:44:53,680
it's very hard to cover
that high dimensional space

885
00:44:53,680 --> 00:44:55,920
with lots of different samples.

886
00:44:55,920 --> 00:44:58,150
So in one dimensions,
something like this

887
00:44:58,150 --> 00:45:00,350
can actually work
really, really well.

888
00:45:00,350 --> 00:45:03,070
But if you can imagine, instead
of this being one-dimensional

889
00:45:03,070 --> 00:45:06,625
or two-dimensional
or 10-dimensional,

890
00:45:06,625 --> 00:45:09,630
you have to have your samples
cover that space well in order

891
00:45:09,630 --> 00:45:12,160
to find a good optimum.

892
00:45:12,160 --> 00:45:17,070

893
00:45:17,070 --> 00:45:19,990
So specifically, what
the planning approach

894
00:45:19,990 --> 00:45:25,310
would look like with a
sampling-based optimization is

895
00:45:25,310 --> 00:45:30,550
you will still run some policy
and train some model, like we

896
00:45:30,550 --> 00:45:32,210
did in the first two steps.

897
00:45:32,210 --> 00:45:35,630
And we'll also be
choosing actions that

898
00:45:35,630 --> 00:45:37,950
are trying to maximize rewards.

899
00:45:37,950 --> 00:45:41,310
So essentially,
things are the same,

900
00:45:41,310 --> 00:45:45,790
except the way that we do this
optimization problem is going

901
00:45:45,790 --> 00:45:51,190
to change, where instead of
running gradient descent,

902
00:45:51,190 --> 00:45:54,030
we could instead sample a
bunch of different actions,

903
00:45:54,030 --> 00:45:56,110
imagine what will
happen in the future

904
00:45:56,110 --> 00:45:59,350
and what rewards
we'll get, and then

905
00:45:59,350 --> 00:46:03,390
potentially refine our
selection of actions

906
00:46:03,390 --> 00:46:06,960
iteratively to then
get a good outcome.

907
00:46:06,960 --> 00:46:10,270

908
00:46:10,270 --> 00:46:12,910
And so in terms
of what this looks

909
00:46:12,910 --> 00:46:17,070
like, if we use capital
A as shorthand to denote

910
00:46:17,070 --> 00:46:24,710
this at to at plus H, then
version 1 is if we just

911
00:46:24,710 --> 00:46:27,030
do one round of
sampling, then we'll

912
00:46:27,030 --> 00:46:30,470
sample a large number of
possible action choices

913
00:46:30,470 --> 00:46:34,110
we could take from
some distribution,

914
00:46:34,110 --> 00:46:41,750
then choose the set of actions
that had the highest reward,

915
00:46:41,750 --> 00:46:44,810
and then run those actions
in the environment.

916
00:46:44,810 --> 00:46:47,390

917
00:46:47,390 --> 00:46:49,848
The technical name for this is
often called random shooting

918
00:46:49,848 --> 00:46:52,432
because you're kind of shooting
out different possible samples

919
00:46:52,432 --> 00:46:55,020
of what could happen in the
future, imagining the future,

920
00:46:55,020 --> 00:47:00,432
evaluating the reward, and then
just picking the one shot that

921
00:47:00,432 --> 00:47:01,390
had the highest reward.

922
00:47:01,390 --> 00:47:04,020

923
00:47:04,020 --> 00:47:08,820
Now, the downside
and the thing that's

924
00:47:08,820 --> 00:47:11,780
kind of unsatisfying
about this first algorithm

925
00:47:11,780 --> 00:47:15,260
is that this distribution
may not be very good.

926
00:47:15,260 --> 00:47:17,932
And so what we
could do is we could

927
00:47:17,932 --> 00:47:20,140
use the cross-entropy method,
where we're iteratively

928
00:47:20,140 --> 00:47:22,340
improving that
distribution, where we start

929
00:47:22,340 --> 00:47:25,580
from some distribution,
maybe a uniform distribution,

930
00:47:25,580 --> 00:47:29,940
evaluate, rank, and pick
the best samples that

931
00:47:29,940 --> 00:47:33,340
have the highest
rewards and then

932
00:47:33,340 --> 00:47:36,660
refit a new distribution
to those best samples

933
00:47:36,660 --> 00:47:39,580
and repeat that process,
where we're then

934
00:47:39,580 --> 00:47:43,740
going to be sampling from
that better distribution

935
00:47:43,740 --> 00:47:49,740
and then evaluating those
samples and repeating.

936
00:47:49,740 --> 00:47:51,580
If this is parallelized,
this process

937
00:47:51,580 --> 00:47:53,660
actually can be quite fast.

938
00:47:53,660 --> 00:47:56,630
If you have
low-dimensional actions,

939
00:47:56,630 --> 00:48:00,052
it's actually also pretty
simple to implement.

940
00:48:00,052 --> 00:48:02,010
But like we talked about,
it doesn't scale well

941
00:48:02,010 --> 00:48:03,730
to high dimensions.

942
00:48:03,730 --> 00:48:08,650
And so it's best if you have a
somewhat low-dimensional space

943
00:48:08,650 --> 00:48:09,830
that you're considering.

944
00:48:09,830 --> 00:48:13,050
This includes both H and
a because the optimization

945
00:48:13,050 --> 00:48:17,570
that you're considering is
over this vector right here.

946
00:48:17,570 --> 00:48:22,010
And the size of this is the
dimensionality of your action

947
00:48:22,010 --> 00:48:23,220
space times the horizon.

948
00:48:23,220 --> 00:48:32,970

949
00:48:32,970 --> 00:48:37,430
Let's run through an example
for roughly how this would work.

950
00:48:37,430 --> 00:48:43,010
So let's say that we are in this
simple, beautiful environment.

951
00:48:43,010 --> 00:48:46,830
And you are trying
to maximize reward.

952
00:48:46,830 --> 00:48:49,370
Maybe you're trying to
get to the highest point.

953
00:48:49,370 --> 00:48:52,850
Because the highest point,
you get the nicest view.

954
00:48:52,850 --> 00:48:56,410
So your reward is just like
the height of where you get to.

955
00:48:56,410 --> 00:48:57,510
You run some policy.

956
00:48:57,510 --> 00:48:59,850
You collect some data like this.

957
00:48:59,850 --> 00:49:04,890
And maybe you learn that when
you train a model on this data,

958
00:49:04,890 --> 00:49:08,725
that when you move right,
then you go higher.

959
00:49:08,725 --> 00:49:10,350
Because generally,
when you move right,

960
00:49:10,350 --> 00:49:14,090
the elevation became higher.

961
00:49:14,090 --> 00:49:20,890
And then when you try to choose
actions according to your model,

962
00:49:20,890 --> 00:49:23,130
then you're going to learn
that you should go right.

963
00:49:23,130 --> 00:49:26,130
So you'll learn that
when you're actually

964
00:49:26,130 --> 00:49:29,460
kind of finding actions
that maximize reward,

965
00:49:29,460 --> 00:49:31,920
your set of actions
will be to go right.

966
00:49:31,920 --> 00:49:35,370

967
00:49:35,370 --> 00:49:39,370
And then you'll actually
execute those actions.

968
00:49:39,370 --> 00:49:42,363
And you'll go right,
which is good for a bit.

969
00:49:42,363 --> 00:49:44,030
You'll be maximizing
reward for a while.

970
00:49:44,030 --> 00:49:47,930
And then at some point,
you'll fall off the cliff.

971
00:49:47,930 --> 00:49:49,030
And that's not so good.

972
00:49:49,030 --> 00:49:51,960

973
00:49:51,960 --> 00:49:54,020
And this is because the
thing that we talked

974
00:49:54,020 --> 00:49:55,520
about at the very
beginning, there's

975
00:49:55,520 --> 00:49:58,720
this data distribution
mismatch where

976
00:49:58,720 --> 00:50:01,280
the data that you
collected at the beginning

977
00:50:01,280 --> 00:50:04,600
doesn't cover the
data that you are

978
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:08,560
taking with your final policy.

979
00:50:08,560 --> 00:50:11,560
And so we have a
simple fix to this.

980
00:50:11,560 --> 00:50:15,400
The simple fix is that when
we then execute actions

981
00:50:15,400 --> 00:50:20,280
in our environment, we can then
add those actions to our data

982
00:50:20,280 --> 00:50:24,180
set and refit our
model to that data set,

983
00:50:24,180 --> 00:50:28,160
learn a more accurate model,
and repeat this process.

984
00:50:28,160 --> 00:50:30,840
So then this is starting to
look a little bit more like

985
00:50:30,840 --> 00:50:33,960
a reinforcement learning
algorithm because we're actually

986
00:50:33,960 --> 00:50:36,960
iteratively collecting data,
fitting a better model--

987
00:50:36,960 --> 00:50:40,400
in this case, a dynamics model--

988
00:50:40,400 --> 00:50:42,920
sampling actions, choosing
actions to execute,

989
00:50:42,920 --> 00:50:45,020
and running those actions
in the environment.

990
00:50:45,020 --> 00:50:47,600

991
00:50:47,600 --> 00:50:51,270
And so then when we do this, we
will then execute the actions

992
00:50:51,270 --> 00:50:52,130
that we planned.

993
00:50:52,130 --> 00:50:53,370
So we'll go off the cliff.

994
00:50:53,370 --> 00:50:56,170
Then we'll learn that our model
will become more accurate.

995
00:50:56,170 --> 00:50:58,770
We'll learn that if we
go right past this point,

996
00:50:58,770 --> 00:51:01,010
we get a lot of
negative rewards.

997
00:51:01,010 --> 00:51:05,230
Or we get a lot of very low
elevation, which corresponds

998
00:51:05,230 --> 00:51:06,550
to negative rewards.

999
00:51:06,550 --> 00:51:10,870
And then we'll be able to learn
a model that goes to the top

1000
00:51:10,870 --> 00:51:11,420
and stops.

1001
00:51:11,420 --> 00:51:17,350

1002
00:51:17,350 --> 00:51:21,070
Now, there is a number of
things that we could still

1003
00:51:21,070 --> 00:51:24,150
improve about this algorithm.

1004
00:51:24,150 --> 00:51:26,990
And let's talk about
a few of those.

1005
00:51:26,990 --> 00:51:35,950
So, so far, we've been
executing this set of actions

1006
00:51:35,950 --> 00:51:38,010
right here in the environment.

1007
00:51:38,010 --> 00:51:40,510
So we've been planning
out a sequence of actions,

1008
00:51:40,510 --> 00:51:41,330
executing them.

1009
00:51:41,330 --> 00:51:50,750
And so what that looks
like is we have our agent.

1010
00:51:50,750 --> 00:51:52,820
And our agent is like--

1011
00:51:52,820 --> 00:51:56,310

1012
00:51:56,310 --> 00:51:59,990
we're running H actions
in the environment--

1013
00:51:59,990 --> 00:52:04,030
so action at, at
plus 1, at plus 2,

1014
00:52:04,030 --> 00:52:08,750
and so forth in
some environment.

1015
00:52:08,750 --> 00:52:10,270
And we're executing
those actions

1016
00:52:10,270 --> 00:52:15,510
without actually having access
to the previous intermediate

1017
00:52:15,510 --> 00:52:17,710
states.

1018
00:52:17,710 --> 00:52:22,230
And one alternative
that you can consider

1019
00:52:22,230 --> 00:52:25,270
is something where
at each time, we're

1020
00:52:25,270 --> 00:52:29,030
going to be planning at to
at plus H. But we could also

1021
00:52:29,030 --> 00:52:37,670
have something where
we plan at to t plus H.

1022
00:52:37,670 --> 00:52:43,790
But we only actually execute
at in the environment.

1023
00:52:43,790 --> 00:52:50,540
And then we then get the
next state back in return.

1024
00:52:50,540 --> 00:52:56,220
And then we replan at the next
time step a new set of actions.

1025
00:52:56,220 --> 00:52:59,180
And so the difference
between these two things--

1026
00:52:59,180 --> 00:53:04,860
this is often what's called open
loop because you're not actually

1027
00:53:04,860 --> 00:53:08,640
using the feedback
from the environment,

1028
00:53:08,640 --> 00:53:15,300
the intermediate st's, when you
are executing future actions,

1029
00:53:15,300 --> 00:53:19,100
whereas this is often
called closed loop

1030
00:53:19,100 --> 00:53:22,700
because you're actually
kind of replanning

1031
00:53:22,700 --> 00:53:24,950
as you get new states.

1032
00:53:24,950 --> 00:53:28,220

1033
00:53:28,220 --> 00:53:30,700
So here's what that looks like.

1034
00:53:30,700 --> 00:53:38,420
Now, I'm curious, does anyone
have thoughts on scenarios

1035
00:53:38,420 --> 00:53:43,060
in which it might be bad
to be open loop like this,

1036
00:53:43,060 --> 00:53:49,460
where you're only planning based
off of st and not planning based

1037
00:53:49,460 --> 00:53:53,160
off of all of the future st's?

1038
00:53:53,160 --> 00:53:55,160
Yeah, so when the environment
is changing a lot.

1039
00:53:55,160 --> 00:53:57,140
So if the environment
changed in a way that

1040
00:53:57,140 --> 00:54:00,020
is different from what you
anticipated because you maybe

1041
00:54:00,020 --> 00:54:02,420
weren't able to predict
everything that would happen,

1042
00:54:02,420 --> 00:54:05,340
then you won't react
to those changes

1043
00:54:05,340 --> 00:54:08,580
when you act in
an open loop way.

1044
00:54:08,580 --> 00:54:10,580
And instead, if
you're closed loop

1045
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:13,980
and you're reconsidering based
off of the latest states,

1046
00:54:13,980 --> 00:54:18,180
then you're able to actually
react to those changes

1047
00:54:18,180 --> 00:54:22,500
and, potentially in
some cases, actually

1048
00:54:22,500 --> 00:54:25,860
have a much, much better
policy, whereas in scenarios

1049
00:54:25,860 --> 00:54:28,680
where the world is a little
bit more deterministic,

1050
00:54:28,680 --> 00:54:33,160
then it can be OK to be a
little bit open loop at times.

1051
00:54:33,160 --> 00:54:36,400
But you usually never want
to have at be too large.

1052
00:54:36,400 --> 00:54:38,400
Otherwise, you're
executing two open loops.

1053
00:54:38,400 --> 00:54:41,860

1054
00:54:41,860 --> 00:54:45,650
Now, we talked about how we can
make planning closed loop, which

1055
00:54:45,650 --> 00:54:52,750
is instead of executing
the entire action sequence,

1056
00:54:52,750 --> 00:54:55,150
you just execute only
the first planned action.

1057
00:54:55,150 --> 00:54:58,130
So we're still going to
plan a sequence of actions

1058
00:54:58,130 --> 00:55:00,790
because we need to consider
not just the first action

1059
00:55:00,790 --> 00:55:03,430
but how all of our actions are
going to affect future rewards.

1060
00:55:03,430 --> 00:55:05,450
But then we'll execute
only the first one,

1061
00:55:05,450 --> 00:55:07,530
observe the resulting state.

1062
00:55:07,530 --> 00:55:12,890
And then we will then replan
an action sequence starting

1063
00:55:12,890 --> 00:55:15,220
at that new next state.

1064
00:55:15,220 --> 00:55:18,890

1065
00:55:18,890 --> 00:55:23,370
And then we can also,
like before, kind of also

1066
00:55:23,370 --> 00:55:28,312
add the data to our data set
and periodically refit our model

1067
00:55:28,312 --> 00:55:29,520
based off of the latest data.

1068
00:55:29,520 --> 00:55:32,215

1069
00:55:32,215 --> 00:55:34,090
This algorithm right
here is something called

1070
00:55:34,090 --> 00:55:36,250
model predictive control.

1071
00:55:36,250 --> 00:55:42,130
It's very, very common in
certain robotic systems,

1072
00:55:42,130 --> 00:55:43,580
very widely used.

1073
00:55:43,580 --> 00:55:49,360
It's also used in other kind
of simple control systems

1074
00:55:49,360 --> 00:55:56,120
as well, where you can model
lower dimensional systems.

1075
00:55:56,120 --> 00:55:58,360
It can be quite effective
for legged robots

1076
00:55:58,360 --> 00:56:03,160
as well, where you're
trying to learn models.

1077
00:56:03,160 --> 00:56:08,360
And you're able to plan
over the next couple seconds

1078
00:56:08,360 --> 00:56:13,997
to figure out what will
lead to good performance

1079
00:56:13,997 --> 00:56:15,330
over the next couple of seconds.

1080
00:56:15,330 --> 00:56:18,400

1081
00:56:18,400 --> 00:56:23,240
And this sort of replanning,
when you actually

1082
00:56:23,240 --> 00:56:25,583
are considering a new plan
at the next time step,

1083
00:56:25,583 --> 00:56:28,000
if something changes or if you
made a mistake, you slipped

1084
00:56:28,000 --> 00:56:29,720
or something, this
replanning allows

1085
00:56:29,720 --> 00:56:31,970
you to actually consider a
completely different course

1086
00:56:31,970 --> 00:56:39,920
of action and, for
example, correct for not

1087
00:56:39,920 --> 00:56:42,300
just for changing environments.

1088
00:56:42,300 --> 00:56:44,800
It can also actually
correct for model errors.

1089
00:56:44,800 --> 00:56:47,520
And so generally,
this sort of approach,

1090
00:56:47,520 --> 00:56:50,700
if your model is accurate in a
large part of the state space

1091
00:56:50,700 --> 00:56:53,457
but in some parts of the
state space, it's inaccurate,

1092
00:56:53,457 --> 00:56:55,040
this sort of approach
ends up actually

1093
00:56:55,040 --> 00:56:58,972
being a lot more robust to
those kinds of model errors.

1094
00:56:58,972 --> 00:57:00,180
Yeah, that's a good question.

1095
00:57:00,180 --> 00:57:02,760
So if there are
multiple correct paths,

1096
00:57:02,760 --> 00:57:07,040
the question is, in that
kind of multi-modality,

1097
00:57:07,040 --> 00:57:11,560
will that lead to oscillation
between those different paths?

1098
00:57:11,560 --> 00:57:14,700
It certainly can lead to that if
you're replanning all the time.

1099
00:57:14,700 --> 00:57:16,760
And you might start
going in one direction.

1100
00:57:16,760 --> 00:57:18,385
And then maybe say
like, oh, maybe I'll

1101
00:57:18,385 --> 00:57:20,360
go in a different direction.

1102
00:57:20,360 --> 00:57:23,600
Sometimes, you also won't
see that if the state kind of

1103
00:57:23,600 --> 00:57:27,058
indicates that you've clearly
committed to one direction.

1104
00:57:27,058 --> 00:57:28,100
So sometimes, you see it.

1105
00:57:28,100 --> 00:57:29,320
Sometimes, you don't.

1106
00:57:29,320 --> 00:57:32,680
The other way to accommodate
this and also actually address

1107
00:57:32,680 --> 00:57:34,640
computational
challenges is that you

1108
00:57:34,640 --> 00:57:39,400
can warm start your plan
for any given time step

1109
00:57:39,400 --> 00:57:42,630
to, basically, the plan
from the previous time step.

1110
00:57:42,630 --> 00:57:46,150
And so if you had
planned at to at plus H

1111
00:57:46,150 --> 00:57:50,830
and that the next time
step you are planning--

1112
00:57:50,830 --> 00:57:55,870
based off of st plus 1,
you're planning at plus 1

1113
00:57:55,870 --> 00:58:00,510
to t plus 1 plus H,
then what you can do

1114
00:58:00,510 --> 00:58:05,670
is you can actually
warm start this using

1115
00:58:05,670 --> 00:58:10,890
everything but the first time
step of this previous plan

1116
00:58:10,890 --> 00:58:12,930
and then randomly
initialize the last action.

1117
00:58:12,930 --> 00:58:16,110
And you'll already have
some kind of initial plan

1118
00:58:16,110 --> 00:58:17,250
that is sensible.

1119
00:58:17,250 --> 00:58:20,550
And so you don't have to run
as long of an optimization

1120
00:58:20,550 --> 00:58:21,210
procedure.

1121
00:58:21,210 --> 00:58:24,630

1122
00:58:24,630 --> 00:58:28,270
And it also helps you commit
to the previous action

1123
00:58:28,270 --> 00:58:31,110
in the previous plan.

1124
00:58:31,110 --> 00:58:31,870
Yeah.

1125
00:58:31,870 --> 00:58:33,870
Instead of randomly
initializing--

1126
00:58:33,870 --> 00:58:36,350
for example, in our
sampling-based thing,

1127
00:58:36,350 --> 00:58:37,790
you could--

1128
00:58:37,790 --> 00:58:40,560
either here or in a
sampling-based approach,

1129
00:58:40,560 --> 00:58:42,760
you could initialize close
to the previous plan.

1130
00:58:42,760 --> 00:58:44,860
You could also add some
noise or add some variance

1131
00:58:44,860 --> 00:58:48,660
to your sampling distribution so
that you're not only considering

1132
00:58:48,660 --> 00:58:49,990
that particular plan.

1133
00:58:49,990 --> 00:58:52,947

1134
00:58:52,947 --> 00:58:55,280
So replanning helps with
non-deterministic environments.

1135
00:58:55,280 --> 00:58:59,260
It also is really, really
useful for model errors.

1136
00:58:59,260 --> 00:59:01,040
It is also compute-intensive.

1137
00:59:01,040 --> 00:59:05,100
So warm starting can be helpful.

1138
00:59:05,100 --> 00:59:07,720
If you're actually replanning
it every single time step,

1139
00:59:07,720 --> 00:59:09,300
it means that every
single time step,

1140
00:59:09,300 --> 00:59:11,860
you're kind of reconsidering
what action to take.

1141
00:59:11,860 --> 00:59:15,900
And so it also can introduce
additional compute challenges

1142
00:59:15,900 --> 00:59:16,900
as well.

1143
00:59:16,900 --> 00:59:19,340
Sometimes, you have something
in the middle where you'll

1144
00:59:19,340 --> 00:59:20,900
have some planning horizon.

1145
00:59:20,900 --> 00:59:25,220
But then maybe you'll replan not
every one step but every three

1146
00:59:25,220 --> 00:59:26,780
steps or every 10
steps or something

1147
00:59:26,780 --> 00:59:28,780
like that, where
maybe your horizon is

1148
00:59:28,780 --> 00:59:31,187
something like 100 time steps.

1149
00:59:31,187 --> 00:59:32,520
So it's an interesting question.

1150
00:59:32,520 --> 00:59:34,853
So the question was like, as
you're learning and getting

1151
00:59:34,853 --> 00:59:37,300
more data, presumably your
model is going to get better.

1152
00:59:37,300 --> 00:59:39,320
And you have more
confidence in your model.

1153
00:59:39,320 --> 00:59:42,740
And so maybe insofar as this
is really useful for correcting

1154
00:59:42,740 --> 00:59:46,780
for model errors, you might be
able to replan less frequently

1155
00:59:46,780 --> 00:59:49,260
when you have a better model.

1156
00:59:49,260 --> 00:59:53,920
So that is certainly
very viable.

1157
00:59:53,920 --> 00:59:55,420
And usually, at
some point, you will

1158
00:59:55,420 --> 00:59:57,620
have some kind of final
model that you're using

1159
00:59:57,620 --> 00:59:58,840
after the learning process.

1160
00:59:58,840 --> 01:00:02,300
And you could use different
hyperparameters with regard

1161
01:00:02,300 --> 01:00:04,620
to replanning for
that final policy

1162
01:00:04,620 --> 01:00:07,180
versus during the
learning process.

1163
01:00:07,180 --> 01:00:10,460
And during the learning process,
it can also be valuable--

1164
01:00:10,460 --> 01:00:13,120
the model errors also help
you explore to some extent

1165
01:00:13,120 --> 01:00:14,620
because you're going
to be exploring

1166
01:00:14,620 --> 01:00:17,020
exactly the regions where
the model is inaccurate

1167
01:00:17,020 --> 01:00:21,500
because you'll be incentivized
to exploit any inaccuracies.

1168
01:00:21,500 --> 01:00:23,900
So the question is,
it seems like this

1169
01:00:23,900 --> 01:00:25,560
is an off-policy algorithm.

1170
01:00:25,560 --> 01:00:31,140
And does it make sense how
do you choose that policy?

1171
01:00:31,140 --> 01:00:33,200
So yes, this is an
off-policy algorithm.

1172
01:00:33,200 --> 01:00:37,810
We don't need to be using
data from our current policy.

1173
01:00:37,810 --> 01:00:39,710
You can actually use
a pretty broad policy.

1174
01:00:39,710 --> 01:00:43,810
And it makes sense to use a
pretty broad policy because then

1175
01:00:43,810 --> 01:00:47,210
you'll get a model that's
accurate in more places,

1176
01:00:47,210 --> 01:00:49,050
assuming that your
model is expressive

1177
01:00:49,050 --> 01:00:51,710
and can fit that broader
data distribution.

1178
01:00:51,710 --> 01:00:54,498

1179
01:00:54,498 --> 01:00:56,495
So far, we've covered
planning with models.

1180
01:00:56,495 --> 01:00:57,870
So we plan a
sequence of actions.

1181
01:00:57,870 --> 01:01:00,850
We can do this either with
gradient descent or sampling.

1182
01:01:00,850 --> 01:01:04,130
We then update the model
using data collected

1183
01:01:04,130 --> 01:01:06,630
through this planning process.

1184
01:01:06,630 --> 01:01:10,970
And we can replan to help
account for mistakes.

1185
01:01:10,970 --> 01:01:15,850
This is a pretty simple
way to use a learned model.

1186
01:01:15,850 --> 01:01:18,250
One of the things that's, I
think, really cool about this

1187
01:01:18,250 --> 01:01:21,170
is if you have a model
that's quite good,

1188
01:01:21,170 --> 01:01:23,330
you can actually plug
in different rewards

1189
01:01:23,330 --> 01:01:26,650
or different goals at
test time even and get

1190
01:01:26,650 --> 01:01:29,410
a policy that can follow--

1191
01:01:29,410 --> 01:01:34,490
for example, if you have a
model that's universally good

1192
01:01:34,490 --> 01:01:37,610
and you have a robot
that's trying to walk, then

1193
01:01:37,610 --> 01:01:39,567
with that same
exact model, you can

1194
01:01:39,567 --> 01:01:41,150
use this process at
test time and say,

1195
01:01:41,150 --> 01:01:42,310
I want you to run forwards.

1196
01:01:42,310 --> 01:01:43,790
I want you to walk backwards.

1197
01:01:43,790 --> 01:01:45,330
I want you to crouch.

1198
01:01:45,330 --> 01:01:50,190
And this kind of model
is quite task-agnostic.

1199
01:01:50,190 --> 01:01:52,615

1200
01:01:52,615 --> 01:01:54,990
The downside is that it's
compute-intensive at test time.

1201
01:01:54,990 --> 01:01:57,390
And so you need to
account for this.

1202
01:01:57,390 --> 01:02:00,230
And if you have an
expensive model,

1203
01:02:00,230 --> 01:02:03,050
an expensive planning
process, this kind

1204
01:02:03,050 --> 01:02:06,850
of planning at test time
might not be suitable.

1205
01:02:06,850 --> 01:02:09,330
And the last thing is we've
been talking about this

1206
01:02:09,330 --> 01:02:11,070
for short horizon problems.

1207
01:02:11,070 --> 01:02:14,410
This is not applicable
to long horizon problems

1208
01:02:14,410 --> 01:02:17,130
unless you have a
shaped reward function.

1209
01:02:17,130 --> 01:02:20,930
So if you have a shaped reward
function that is telling you

1210
01:02:20,930 --> 01:02:24,250
a lot about what a good state
is versus what a bad state is--

1211
01:02:24,250 --> 01:02:27,450
for example, maybe you have
an infinite horizon problem

1212
01:02:27,450 --> 01:02:29,175
that you want to
walk forward forever.

1213
01:02:29,175 --> 01:02:31,550
You want to learn a policy
that can walk forward forever.

1214
01:02:31,550 --> 01:02:33,258
But you have a very
dense reward function

1215
01:02:33,258 --> 01:02:35,520
that's like forward velocity.

1216
01:02:35,520 --> 01:02:37,640
Then you can plan
for a short horizon

1217
01:02:37,640 --> 01:02:42,040
in a way that approximates
the long horizon problem.

1218
01:02:42,040 --> 01:02:44,640
But if you have a long
horizon problem that

1219
01:02:44,640 --> 01:02:47,080
doesn't have a very
dense reward signal,

1220
01:02:47,080 --> 01:02:48,850
then this is not
a good approach.

1221
01:02:48,850 --> 01:02:52,040

1222
01:02:52,040 --> 01:02:57,100
And part of the reason for this
is the accuracy of the model.

1223
01:02:57,100 --> 01:02:59,600
Another part of
this is that it's

1224
01:02:59,600 --> 01:03:03,280
going to be expensive to be
planning out a very long horizon

1225
01:03:03,280 --> 01:03:07,000
sequence of actions
because you're not

1226
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:08,940
learning a policy in this case.

1227
01:03:08,940 --> 01:03:15,590

1228
01:03:15,590 --> 01:03:20,480
Now, the question is, can we
train a policy using this model?

1229
01:03:20,480 --> 01:03:24,440
And the first option here
is you could actually

1230
01:03:24,440 --> 01:03:27,140
distill a planner's
actions into a policy.

1231
01:03:27,140 --> 01:03:30,440
So you could store these
actions and then train a policy

1232
01:03:30,440 --> 01:03:31,410
to match these actions.

1233
01:03:31,410 --> 01:03:35,414

1234
01:03:35,414 --> 01:03:38,262
This would address the
compute-intensive problem.

1235
01:03:38,262 --> 01:03:40,470
And this would make it a
lot cheaper because then you

1236
01:03:40,470 --> 01:03:42,887
just need to run a forward
pass through the neural network

1237
01:03:42,887 --> 01:03:45,510
to get your actions.

1238
01:03:45,510 --> 01:03:48,290
This doesn't address the
horizon problem, though,

1239
01:03:48,290 --> 01:03:51,590
because we're still going
to be using this procedure

1240
01:03:51,590 --> 01:03:55,150
to optimize for actions.

1241
01:03:55,150 --> 01:03:57,310
And then in terms
of using models

1242
01:03:57,310 --> 01:04:01,490
for longer horizon problems,
there's two main solutions.

1243
01:04:01,490 --> 01:04:04,630

1244
01:04:04,630 --> 01:04:07,870
The first solution is
that right now, we're

1245
01:04:07,870 --> 01:04:14,590
optimizing for a sum of
rewards from t to t plus H.

1246
01:04:14,590 --> 01:04:18,390
And if we want to optimize for
a longer horizon of rewards,

1247
01:04:18,390 --> 01:04:20,710
so far the way
that we would have

1248
01:04:20,710 --> 01:04:24,290
to do that is by optimizing over
a longer horizon of actions,

1249
01:04:24,290 --> 01:04:26,812
which gets higher and
higher dimensional.

1250
01:04:26,812 --> 01:04:28,270
But another thing
that you could do

1251
01:04:28,270 --> 01:04:33,430
is that if you have a value
function or an estimate

1252
01:04:33,430 --> 01:04:35,550
of a value function,
then you could actually

1253
01:04:35,550 --> 01:04:39,630
use that value function to help
this process, where instead

1254
01:04:39,630 --> 01:04:43,750
of trying to maximize the sum
of rewards for the next H time

1255
01:04:43,750 --> 01:04:46,270
steps, you could optimize
for the sum of rewards

1256
01:04:46,270 --> 01:04:51,630
for the next time steps plus
your estimate of the value

1257
01:04:51,630 --> 01:04:58,790
of the state that you end up at
after taking those H actions.

1258
01:04:58,790 --> 01:05:02,910
And so this would end
up with an algorithm

1259
01:05:02,910 --> 01:05:05,950
where you're learning both a
model and a value function.

1260
01:05:05,950 --> 01:05:09,028
And then you would be running
this planning process.

1261
01:05:09,028 --> 01:05:11,070
You'd be considering a
bunch of different actions

1262
01:05:11,070 --> 01:05:13,745
over some horizon and
then pass that horizon.

1263
01:05:13,745 --> 01:05:15,370
You're just asking
your value function,

1264
01:05:15,370 --> 01:05:17,870
is that a good state
or a bad state?

1265
01:05:17,870 --> 01:05:23,510
And so this is a valid
way to combine planning

1266
01:05:23,510 --> 01:05:31,020
with value-based RL in
order to select actions.

1267
01:05:31,020 --> 01:05:34,240
So the question is,
why use the model?

1268
01:05:34,240 --> 01:05:37,400
Why not just use a
Q-function, for example?

1269
01:05:37,400 --> 01:05:40,340

1270
01:05:40,340 --> 01:05:42,340
This is why I didn't want
to spend too much time

1271
01:05:42,340 --> 01:05:46,460
on this method, is that in this
case, you have to learn a value

1272
01:05:46,460 --> 01:05:47,620
function and a model.

1273
01:05:47,620 --> 01:05:49,370
You're learning a lot
of different things.

1274
01:05:49,370 --> 01:05:51,500
And there's a lot
of moving pieces.

1275
01:05:51,500 --> 01:05:53,640
There is still some
merit to using this.

1276
01:05:53,640 --> 01:05:57,300
And for example, if
you know the model,

1277
01:05:57,300 --> 01:05:59,260
then you're going
to get a better

1278
01:05:59,260 --> 01:06:02,920
estimate of future rewards
than your value function.

1279
01:06:02,920 --> 01:06:07,300
And so you can actually use
this to plan out future actions.

1280
01:06:07,300 --> 01:06:09,460
Like, in games, it's
actually really common

1281
01:06:09,460 --> 01:06:12,980
to do these rollouts
through your model

1282
01:06:12,980 --> 01:06:18,180
of the game to the extent that
it's computationally feasible

1283
01:06:18,180 --> 01:06:21,757
and then use your value
function just because it's

1284
01:06:21,757 --> 01:06:22,840
going to be more accurate.

1285
01:06:22,840 --> 01:06:24,507
It's going to give
you a better estimate

1286
01:06:24,507 --> 01:06:27,075
of how good your action is.

1287
01:06:27,075 --> 01:06:28,950
But it's only if you
have a really good model

1288
01:06:28,950 --> 01:06:34,210
or if it's practical and not too
difficult to learn a good model.

1289
01:06:34,210 --> 01:06:40,063
And then the other approach,
which I'm thinking--

1290
01:06:40,063 --> 01:06:41,230
we don't have too much time.

1291
01:06:41,230 --> 01:06:50,810
And so I think I might cover
this second approach on Friday

1292
01:06:50,810 --> 01:06:58,050
and then go through
the case study.

1293
01:06:58,050 --> 01:07:06,630
So let's talk about
this case study.

1294
01:07:06,630 --> 01:07:08,130
So this case study
is actually going

1295
01:07:08,130 --> 01:07:11,890
to be an example
of using planning

1296
01:07:11,890 --> 01:07:15,090
for dexterous manipulation.

1297
01:07:15,090 --> 01:07:18,890
And the reason why I want to
cover this case study briefly

1298
01:07:18,890 --> 01:07:21,010
is that in my mind,
it's actually still

1299
01:07:21,010 --> 01:07:22,970
one of the most impressive
robotics results

1300
01:07:22,970 --> 01:07:24,970
with a five-fingered hand.

1301
01:07:24,970 --> 01:07:27,610
And it also has some
pretty nice comparisons

1302
01:07:27,610 --> 01:07:29,730
that are elucidating
about what is

1303
01:07:29,730 --> 01:07:34,970
important for good performance
in model-based RL and planning.

1304
01:07:34,970 --> 01:07:37,010
So the way it
works is the states

1305
01:07:37,010 --> 01:07:40,850
are the position of the hand
and the position of objects.

1306
01:07:40,850 --> 01:07:45,110
This assumes that you know
the 3D position of an object,

1307
01:07:45,110 --> 01:07:49,662
which you can estimate with a
computer vision system or put

1308
01:07:49,662 --> 01:07:54,450
motion capture markers
on the objects,

1309
01:07:54,450 --> 01:07:57,090
whereas the hand position, you
can use sensors in the hand

1310
01:07:57,090 --> 01:08:00,170
itself in order to measure
the hand positions.

1311
01:08:00,170 --> 01:08:02,350
The action space is to
control a five-fingered hand.

1312
01:08:02,350 --> 01:08:07,010
It has 24 different joints
that you can control.

1313
01:08:07,010 --> 01:08:12,010
And the reward is to target some
kind of object trajectory, where

1314
01:08:12,010 --> 01:08:14,360
there's also some penalty
for dropping the object.

1315
01:08:14,360 --> 01:08:17,370

1316
01:08:17,370 --> 01:08:21,210
The model is going to be
actually pretty simple.

1317
01:08:21,210 --> 01:08:23,770
The model is going to be a
neural network with two hidden

1318
01:08:23,770 --> 01:08:26,040
layers of size 500.

1319
01:08:26,040 --> 01:08:29,439
But it's also going to train
an ensemble of neural networks.

1320
01:08:29,439 --> 01:08:32,479
And this is an
example that I wanted

1321
01:08:32,479 --> 01:08:34,680
to look at because this
kind of ensembling approach

1322
01:08:34,680 --> 01:08:36,396
can also help with model errors.

1323
01:08:36,396 --> 01:08:38,479
And the way this works is
instead of just training

1324
01:08:38,479 --> 01:08:40,760
one neural network, you'll
train three neural networks

1325
01:08:40,760 --> 01:08:42,600
with different random
initializations

1326
01:08:42,600 --> 01:08:46,040
and different kind of
mini batch sampling, just

1327
01:08:46,040 --> 01:08:47,680
with different random seeds.

1328
01:08:47,680 --> 01:08:51,040
And then if one model is
very optimistic about what

1329
01:08:51,040 --> 01:08:53,200
will happen, that can
be counterbalanced

1330
01:08:53,200 --> 01:08:55,899
by errors in other models.

1331
01:08:55,899 --> 01:09:02,840
And it essentially gives you
a way to deal with the fact

1332
01:09:02,840 --> 01:09:08,160
that models will have different
errors in different ways.

1333
01:09:08,160 --> 01:09:12,399
So you're not going to exploit
the errors in any one model.

1334
01:09:12,399 --> 01:09:14,560
And then the planner
is a modified version

1335
01:09:14,560 --> 01:09:15,819
of the cross-entropy method.

1336
01:09:15,819 --> 01:09:18,960
So it's going to be using
iterative resampling

1337
01:09:18,960 --> 01:09:20,439
of the actions.

1338
01:09:20,439 --> 01:09:25,720
And the modifications
are that it's

1339
01:09:25,720 --> 01:09:29,540
going to use this softer
reward weighted mean.

1340
01:09:29,540 --> 01:09:31,000
And it does some
sort of temporal

1341
01:09:31,000 --> 01:09:33,760
smoothing on the actions
to encourage and search

1342
01:09:33,760 --> 01:09:41,064
over actions that are taking
smoother trajectories.

1343
01:09:41,064 --> 01:09:42,439
But you can
basically think of it

1344
01:09:42,439 --> 01:09:44,040
as cross-entropy
method or this kind

1345
01:09:44,040 --> 01:09:46,560
of iterative sampling-based
optimization.

1346
01:09:46,560 --> 01:09:49,319
And then they alternate
between, in step 1,

1347
01:09:49,319 --> 01:09:54,930
collecting 30 trajectories
and updating the model.

1348
01:09:54,930 --> 01:09:57,920

1349
01:09:57,920 --> 01:09:59,840
So then in the
simulated experiments,

1350
01:09:59,840 --> 01:10:02,740
we are going to see a comparison
to some model free methods,

1351
01:10:02,740 --> 01:10:05,277
methods that are not
using a dynamics model.

1352
01:10:05,277 --> 01:10:07,360
We'll see [? soft ?] actor
critic and an algorithm

1353
01:10:07,360 --> 01:10:09,800
called natural policy gradient,
which is a policy gradient

1354
01:10:09,800 --> 01:10:10,760
method.

1355
01:10:10,760 --> 01:10:12,960
And then we'll also see
some model-based methods.

1356
01:10:12,960 --> 01:10:16,040
So the proposed
method is called PDDM.

1357
01:10:16,040 --> 01:10:18,200
We'll also see an
algorithm called

1358
01:10:18,200 --> 01:10:23,030
MBPO, a different kind
of planner called PETS,

1359
01:10:23,030 --> 01:10:25,630
and then also a method
that doesn't use ensembles

1360
01:10:25,630 --> 01:10:29,110
and doesn't use
iterative sampling.

1361
01:10:29,110 --> 01:10:31,510
And here are some
videos of what you're

1362
01:10:31,510 --> 01:10:34,130
able to do with this method.

1363
01:10:34,130 --> 01:10:40,710
So we're running planning in
the loop of actually execution.

1364
01:10:40,710 --> 01:10:44,110
And we see that
it's able to learn

1365
01:10:44,110 --> 01:10:47,030
how to rotate the
bounding balls in the hand

1366
01:10:47,030 --> 01:10:52,110
and also write digits
on this piece of paper.

1367
01:10:52,110 --> 01:10:56,430
And we see that if we plot the
kind of reward where higher

1368
01:10:56,430 --> 01:10:59,270
is better over the
number of data points

1369
01:10:59,270 --> 01:11:01,670
that are collected in
order to fit the model

1370
01:11:01,670 --> 01:11:05,030
or in the policy in the
case of model free methods,

1371
01:11:05,030 --> 01:11:08,230
we see that, first, for this
really complex task, only

1372
01:11:08,230 --> 01:11:10,590
the proposed method is able
to solve it because it's

1373
01:11:10,590 --> 01:11:12,242
really challenging.

1374
01:11:12,242 --> 01:11:14,950
And we also see that some of the
methods that, for example, don't

1375
01:11:14,950 --> 01:11:17,790
do iterative sampling,
that, for example, just do

1376
01:11:17,790 --> 01:11:19,990
random sampling,
shown in yellow,

1377
01:11:19,990 --> 01:11:22,340
get very poor performance
because you're not

1378
01:11:22,340 --> 01:11:26,180
able to get to a good
part of the distribution

1379
01:11:26,180 --> 01:11:28,900
and hone in on good actions.

1380
01:11:28,900 --> 01:11:32,340
And in the case of handwriting,
which is a little bit easier,

1381
01:11:32,340 --> 01:11:35,180
we see that some methods are
able to get good performance.

1382
01:11:35,180 --> 01:11:38,720
SAC and natural policy
gradient get high performance.

1383
01:11:38,720 --> 01:11:42,960
But they need far, far more data
than this model-based method,

1384
01:11:42,960 --> 01:11:44,540
which is shown in green.

1385
01:11:44,540 --> 01:11:48,460
And so the model-based method
is using maybe on the order

1386
01:11:48,460 --> 01:11:53,860
of 100,000 time steps of data,
whereas the model-free methods

1387
01:11:53,860 --> 01:11:57,232
are using half a
million time steps.

1388
01:11:57,232 --> 01:11:59,440
So it's a lot more efficient
than model-free methods.

1389
01:11:59,440 --> 01:12:03,340
It's also more performant than
other model-based methods.

1390
01:12:03,340 --> 01:12:06,520
We also see that the model
needs to be sufficiently large.

1391
01:12:06,520 --> 01:12:10,100
You also need this ensemble
for good performance.

1392
01:12:10,100 --> 01:12:12,700
And there's different trade-offs
with regard to the planning

1393
01:12:12,700 --> 01:12:13,240
horizon.

1394
01:12:13,240 --> 01:12:14,615
So you don't want
to plan for too

1395
01:12:14,615 --> 01:12:18,140
long into the future because
that's too high dimensional.

1396
01:12:18,140 --> 01:12:22,380
And then they also did it on
a real hand using the method

1397
01:12:22,380 --> 01:12:24,460
that we talked about.

1398
01:12:24,460 --> 01:12:27,300
Data efficiency is really
important for fragile hardware.

1399
01:12:27,300 --> 01:12:29,387
This robot hand is very fragile.

1400
01:12:29,387 --> 01:12:31,720
There actually is one at
Stanford in the Gates Building.

1401
01:12:31,720 --> 01:12:34,387
And I think all the students are
afraid to touch it because they

1402
01:12:34,387 --> 01:12:35,353
don't want to break it.

1403
01:12:35,353 --> 01:12:38,020
And then they have to figure out
how to fix it because it's also

1404
01:12:38,020 --> 01:12:40,940
really complicated.

1405
01:12:40,940 --> 01:12:44,780
And the success rate for
90-degree turns is actually

1406
01:12:44,780 --> 01:12:48,300
around 100% after just
four hours of data.

1407
01:12:48,300 --> 01:12:51,827
For 180-degree turns,
which is a bit harder,

1408
01:12:51,827 --> 01:12:53,160
the success rate is a bit lower.

1409
01:12:53,160 --> 01:12:55,368
But you're still able to
make significant performance

1410
01:12:55,368 --> 01:12:57,060
with four hours of data.

1411
01:12:57,060 --> 01:13:01,580
Also, they reset the system
when it dropped a ball using

1412
01:13:01,580 --> 01:13:04,290
another robot, like this setup.

1413
01:13:04,290 --> 01:13:07,660

1414
01:13:07,660 --> 01:13:08,820
That's it for today.

1415
01:13:08,820 --> 01:13:12,780
Next time, we'll cover the last
part about model-based RL and RL

1416
01:13:12,780 --> 01:13:15,290
with multiple tasks and goals.

